WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.960
Tim Ferriss: I usually have a pretty good idea of&nbsp;
where I'm going to start these conversations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:31.480
I'm looking at this list and it's a combination&nbsp;
of Memento meets Forrest Gump meets globetrotting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:38.720
and I'm going to let you choose. So should we&nbsp;
start with — and this will be very back and forth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.800
we're going to fast-forward, rewind,&nbsp;
so we can start at any point really.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.360
Charlie Tuna or why you paid $25,000 to&nbsp;
buy fuckyourself.com just to get an email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.160
address, or Pilgrims? Where should we start?
Andrew Rosener: Whoa. All right, let's start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:05.600
with Charlie Tuna because it inspires all else.
Tim Ferriss: Perfect. Who the hell or what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.480
the hell is Charlie Tuna?
Andrew Rosener: In college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.640
I studied management information systems, which&nbsp;
was basically a hybrid of business and computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:23.760
science. I chose that because quite pragmatically&nbsp;
as I am, it was at that time, mid-'90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.800
the highest paying job out of college. And so I&nbsp;
said, "Well, that's what I'm going to do." So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:36.320
did that and I started a software business and in&nbsp;
East Hampton as a matter of fact. And I quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.480
realized that it was not what I want to do with&nbsp;
my life, and so I then subsequently graduated a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.640
few months later after coming to that realization&nbsp;
with a degree in something I didn't want to do and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.600
I had no idea what I was going to do with my&nbsp;
life. And I got a call from so-called Charlie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.520
Tuna, a very, very close friend, somebody I would&nbsp;
call a mentor, and an extraordinarily unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.400
individual on this planet, Peter Moehrke. So I&nbsp;
get a call from Peter Moehrke, and Peter says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.640
"Do you want to come sell fish?"
Tim Ferriss: How did he get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.440
your name or number or both?
Andrew Rosener: So Peter contacted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.400
University of Rhode Island where I went to school.&nbsp;
He contacted the business school and he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.920
"I'm looking for an entrepreneurial go-getter&nbsp;
to become my Mini-Me." And Peter wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.427
retire and he wanted to train somebody that&nbsp;
had not come from the seafood industry.&nbsp;

00:02:24.427 --> 00:02:27.864
Tim Ferriss: That's what we&nbsp;
call foreshadowing, folks.&nbsp;

00:02:27.864 --> 00:02:35.720
Andrew Rosener: He wanted to train somebody to&nbsp;
basically take over the business in his own image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.920
and I was the chosen one. So anyways,&nbsp;
I basically told him to go pound sand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.720
and then at that point he offered me $1,000 to&nbsp;
come hang out with him for the day in Newport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:52.000
Rhode Island. And I did, and it was kind of like&nbsp;
love at first sight, we hit it off. I walked into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.320
the office in a business suit with a tie on&nbsp;
and a briefcase, my resume in the briefcase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.400
and the secretary let me into his office, and he&nbsp;
had his feet up on the desk and he was blowing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.720
a bong hit. And he looked at me and he said, "If&nbsp;
you ever come in my office in a suit again, it'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.824
be the last time you ever come in my office."
Tim Ferriss: What was he wearing?&nbsp;

00:03:10.824 --> 00:03:19.360
Andrew Rosener: He was wearing a Native American&nbsp;
poncho type of thing. And he's got long hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:27.560
with a ponytail and this unique beard that, I&nbsp;
don't know, only Peter could carry. But he's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:33.560
brilliant man. He's a Stanford grad, he's an old&nbsp;
hippie, and he just has a unique perspective on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:39.520
the world. And I've never encountered anybody&nbsp;
else that sees the world the way that he does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.360
And I think it was probably the most important&nbsp;
event of my life because I'm ambitious and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:50.480
I'm aggressive and I'm full of energy and&nbsp;
ready to go tear the ass out of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.600
but without any real direction.
Tim Ferriss: Isn't always the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.560
weapon suited to the task?
Andrew Rosener: Absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:04.880
not. Absolutely not. But at that age, it's&nbsp;
better than nothing. And Peter was very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.385
good at harnessing that and pointing it in&nbsp;
certain directions. Directions that were —&nbsp;

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:09.920
Tim Ferriss: Beneficial to Peter.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. So I went under Peter's wing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.640
and I spent eight years in the seafood business,&nbsp;
the frozen seafood business. I traveled the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:21.240
I negotiated with I couldn't even imagine the&nbsp;
number of different countries and the types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.680
people. I literally did business with some of&nbsp;
the worst people on earth. The fish business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:32.760
is a cash business basically worldwide. And&nbsp;
so it lends itself to certain elements and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:37.200
it was just an amazing life experience.
Tim Ferriss: So walk me through what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.120
looks like, let's just say your first week or&nbsp;
month on the job. How is the compensation set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.840
up and what are you actually trying to do?
Andrew Rosener: So my first day on the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.960
I come into the office, obviously no longer&nbsp;
wearing a business suit, and I sit down at my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.920
designated desk. Now there's only about, I don't&nbsp;
know, at that time, four or five people. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:02.360
I sit down at my desk and I don't know, an hour&nbsp;
or two later, Peter comes out of his office and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.960
he comes over to me and I'm basically sitting&nbsp;
there doing nothing. I've got some reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:13.680
material that somebody's given me to look over,&nbsp;
and he walks over to my desk and he points at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:20.560
poster on the wall that says "Exactitude." And he&nbsp;
says, "Do you know what that means?" And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.720
"No." And he said, "Have you ever read the&nbsp;
book Atlas Shrugged?" I said, "No." He said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:31.720
"Have you ever heard of it?" I said, "No." He&nbsp;
says, "Do you know who Ayn Rand is?" I said, "No."&nbsp;

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.400
Went into his office without saying a word. Went&nbsp;
into his office, got a copy of Atlas Shrugged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.800
brought it back to me, and he said, "Go home now."&nbsp;
And I was like, "Wait, what are you saying?" He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.560
like, "Go read this book. Don't come back&nbsp;
in until you're done." So I literally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:50.307
I went home and I literally took about five&nbsp;
days to read the book. It's a big book.&nbsp;

00:05:50.307 --> 00:05:52.640
Tim Ferriss: It's not a short book.
Andrew Rosener: No, it's not. It's a big book, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:06:01.920
it's not easy reading either. At least the first&nbsp;
200 pages, it's a 200-page intro. And so I read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:07.707
the book, I came back in, it was kind of like a&nbsp;
fifth grade, what do you call that? Like a book —&nbsp;

00:06:07.707 --> 00:06:11.400
Tim Ferriss: Book report?
Andrew Rosener: Book review. And I don't know, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.440
guess I passed. That was it. And then it was like,&nbsp;
"Okay, look, come with me." And he took me down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.800
to New Bedford, Massachusetts and drove me around&nbsp;
to these different scallop-processing facilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:29.160
And I met some absolutely crazy people. Amazing,&nbsp;
amazing people. These are just truly — to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:35.400
day, I was just actually back in Rhode Island the&nbsp;
end of September for a little memorial thing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:40.840
I met up with some of these guys that I hadn't&nbsp;
seen in a long time. And it brought me right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:45.240
back. Really just amazing people. You just don't&nbsp;
— I'm sure there's other industries like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:52.320
but seafood is a really special, truly&nbsp;
salt-of-the-earth business and it's such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.800
dichotomy from the business I'm in today.
Tim Ferriss: In that boiler room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:01.520
what did exactitude mean? Why was it on the wall?
Andrew Rosener: I think it means different things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.984
to different people. To Peter —
Tim Ferriss: To Peter.&nbsp;

00:07:03.984 --> 00:07:09.600
Andrew Rosener: What it meant was&nbsp;
having the right information in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:15.680
order to arrive at the right answer.&nbsp;
He did not like knee-jerk reactions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.200
He didn't like knee-jerk answers or imprecision.
Tim Ferriss: So once you've read Atlas Shrugged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.360
which I'll admit I've not read, but —
Andrew Rosener: Oh, must.&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:32.280
Tim Ferriss: I know. So shame unto me. Once you've&nbsp;
read that, you come back five days later, at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:36.707
point, is it some form of arbitrage? What is the —
Andrew Rosener: Basically.&nbsp;

00:07:36.707 --> 00:07:38.120
Tim Ferriss: Business model?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah, basically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:45.040
So what's remarkable is Peter's business is&nbsp;
still going today. I think it's got to be 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.000
years now. I think it was 30 years — it's got&nbsp;
to be 40 years now. I think he started in 1985,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:55.800
so roughly 40 years, 38 years. He doesn't own a&nbsp;
factory, he doesn't process, he doesn't own boats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:01.520
He's primarily scallops. There's other businesses,&nbsp;
but its, let's say, core business is scallops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:07.000
and all of his competition, 100 percent of&nbsp;
his competition, at a minimum, they own the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:13.800
processing plant. Most of them own boats, most&nbsp;
of them have joint ventures or partnerships with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:22.080
foreign processors, aquaculture, et cetera. And&nbsp;
despite these seeming disadvantages, Peter has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:29.360
been nimble and he's kept his overhead low and&nbsp;
through the good times and the bad, he's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.944
kept that business running. And in great times —
Tim Ferriss: And is he buying and then reselling?&nbsp;

00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:38.000
Andrew Rosener: Buying, putting in his packaging,&nbsp;
in some cases — I don't know if we want to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.480
into that today, but let's call it value-add.
Tim Ferriss: We can skip certain details if —&nbsp;

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:51.480
Andrew Rosener: They would say they're&nbsp;
value-adding and repackaging and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:56.800
reselling. And it's pure arbitrage.
Tim Ferriss: So what was the annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.600
turnover? And I'm going to hop to&nbsp;
a specific example. So what was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.640
annual revenues roughly when you started there?
Andrew Rosener: They were doing about seven or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:11.744
eight million a year when I started.
Tim Ferriss: And how did it grow?&nbsp;

00:09:11.744 --> 00:09:18.640
Andrew Rosener: So quite quickly, we realized&nbsp;
I was good at this game and I became the vice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:26.320
president of sales. And I think when we left, we&nbsp;
were about 35,000,000, which is a lot of scallops.&nbsp;

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:30.484
Tim Ferriss: How do Hokkaido&nbsp;
scallops fit into this picture?&nbsp;

00:09:30.484 --> 00:09:31.240
Andrew Rosener: So —
Tim Ferriss: Northern island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:36.320
of Japan, for people who may not know.
Andrew Rosener: A couple of years in — so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:42.440
basically, this is a pure sales game. So I&nbsp;
literally have the equivalent of a phone book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:47.480
but it's the who's who of the seafood&nbsp;
industry. And I'm literally making about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:51.983
I don't know, 150 calls every day. And&nbsp;
it's just cold calling, cold calling.&nbsp;

00:09:51.983 --> 00:09:54.464
Tim Ferriss: And you're selling to&nbsp;
distributors, to restaurants, to —&nbsp;

00:09:54.464 --> 00:09:59.120
Andrew Rosener: No, no restaurants. Primarily&nbsp;
distributors, other wholesalers, restaurant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.541
supply companies, cruise lines.
Tim Ferriss: Got it.&nbsp;

00:10:01.541 --> 00:10:05.160
Andrew Rosener: So I say no restaurants, but large&nbsp;
restaurant chains, which is where we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:13.400
with this story. But a couple years in, I come&nbsp;
across Benihana and I call up the Fort Lauderdale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:18.880
headquarters at Benihana and, "You guys want to&nbsp;
buy some scallops?" And it's just pure luck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:24.760
they happened to be in a transition period where&nbsp;
they'd been getting all of their seafood from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:31.640
one company and they decided that they could get&nbsp;
better pricing and there was other advantages by —&nbsp;

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:34.680
Tim Ferriss: Put out a bid.
Andrew Rosener: Put out a bid for each individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:42.400
product. And so it was just great timing.&nbsp;
And so I had the opportunity to put in a bid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:49.560
and by any stretch of the imagination, I shouldn't&nbsp;
have had any chance of winning the contract. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:58.440
as fate would have it, I did my due diligence&nbsp;
on the buyer, and he was Nicaraguan by descent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:05.200
and he loved cigars. And Peter happened to be&nbsp;
a cigar connoisseur, and so he sent me to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:15.160
guy who could basically source any cigar. I said,&nbsp;
"Look, I'm looking for an amazing, amazing cigar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:19.920
that if it happened to be from Nicaragua, might&nbsp;
even give me an extra edge, but something that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.920
going to be really wow." And he said, "I've&nbsp;
got a cigar for you." Now, I couldn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:28.960
tell you what that cigar is, but it was a very&nbsp;
expensive, very good cigar. I got a box of them.&nbsp;

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:32.840
And so I flew down to Fort Lauderdale and I&nbsp;
took this guy out to dinner, and I gave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:39.600
this box of cigars. And the fact that I knew he&nbsp;
was from Nicaragua, the fact that I happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:45.320
know this — I didn't actually know, but he thought&nbsp;
I did — this brand of cigars, it was literally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:54.080
I nailed it. And so we had an amazing dinner&nbsp;
and we drank way too much. And ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:59.360
I made this sales pitch about Hokkaido scallops. I&nbsp;
said, "Look, you guys are using American scallops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:06.880
and American scallops are great. Arguably, if&nbsp;
you're getting them pure form, which it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:11.584
difficult, it's the best in the world. However —
Tim Ferriss: Pure form, meaning unadulterated?&nbsp;

00:12:11.584 --> 00:12:14.253
Andrew Rosener: Unadulterated,&nbsp;
within a couple of days.&nbsp;

00:12:14.253 --> 00:12:16.744
Tim Ferriss: Not imitation&nbsp;
crab meat type of scallop?&nbsp;

00:12:16.744 --> 00:12:20.880
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. Yeah. That's&nbsp;
the least of your worries. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.480
the value-adding part that's not so —
Tim Ferriss: You're eating Guinea pigs.&nbsp;

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:30.440
Andrew Rosener: So the interesting part of this&nbsp;
was basically, American scallops are graded zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:36.480
to 10, 10 to 20, 20 to 30. And that's basically&nbsp;
zero to 10 per pound, 10 to 20 per pound, 20 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:43.560
30 per pound. But the Japanese, as they do, grade&nbsp;
them much more consistently, and so it's zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:51.120
to two, two to four, four to six, six to eight,&nbsp;
eight to 10. And that tightness of grading makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:58.480
an enormous difference if you are a restaurant and&nbsp;
you're trying to calculate your plate cost. So you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:03.440
want your plates to be consistent, you can't be,&nbsp;
"That one plate has four scallops, one plate's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:10.640
got six, and oh, well, I've got this one giant&nbsp;
scallop, and so now my plate cost is thrown off."&nbsp;

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:18.280
So that inconsistency, and scallops are one of,&nbsp;
if not the most expensive per-pound protein from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:25.640
the sea. And so it makes a big difference. And so&nbsp;
I basically made this whole pitch about, "Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:33.560
you guys can have consistent plating, consistent&nbsp;
plate cost." And there was a lot more to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:39.200
but that message resonated and it closed the&nbsp;
deal and I got the contract. And it literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:45.280
became our largest client, and I supplied all&nbsp;
of Benihana worldwide, all their scallops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:51.120
and I subsequently became basically the&nbsp;
world's leading expert on Hokkaido scallops.&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:56.600
Tim Ferriss: So was it a known&nbsp;
fact in the industry that Hokkaido&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:00.427
scallops had these advantages?
Andrew Rosener: No. Nobody cared.&nbsp;

00:14:00.427 --> 00:14:01.680
Tim Ferriss: So how did you —
Andrew Rosener: Nobody cared because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:08.160
were taking the packaging that came from Japan,&nbsp;
and they were just repacking it into American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:13.760
standards. And so it was just another source, just&nbsp;
like we got scallops from China, we got scallops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:21.828
from Chile, from Peru. It was just another origin.&nbsp;
High quality but the grading wasn't a selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.828 --> 00:14:26.040
point. That wasn't a known selling point.
Tim Ferriss: So what made you good? We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:30.760
spent a good amount of time together, so I&nbsp;
have some guesses, but rather than guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:35.280
what do you think made you good at this job?
Andrew Rosener: I'm not afraid to sell. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:43.000
think I have to start there, is that I've just — I&nbsp;
took it for granted that, I don't know, "Oh, hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.840
me a phone book. I'm going to start making cold&nbsp;
calls." I'm going to get on the phone with people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:49.224
try and sell them something that they —
Tim Ferriss: Never knew they needed.&nbsp;

00:14:49.224 --> 00:14:55.680
Andrew Rosener: Never knew they needed. I think&nbsp;
that was a big factor. And then I've got the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:02.400
gift of gab. I'm rarely at a loss for words. I'm&nbsp;
very good at adapting to people and meeting people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:08.227
where they are. Whereas I think a lot of people in&nbsp;
sales are trying to bring somebody over to where —&nbsp;

00:15:08.227 --> 00:15:09.200
Tim Ferriss: Where they are.
Andrew Rosener: Where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:14.320
are. You got to meet the person where&nbsp;
they are and understand what they need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:19.200
and then craft your message and craft whatever it&nbsp;
is you're selling, if possible, to meet them where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:24.760
they are for the need that they may not even know&nbsp;
they have, but you've identified. So I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:31.720
adaptability. Peter had two nicknames for&nbsp;
me. "The Chameleon" was one, and "Crystal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:40.920
Meth" was the other. I have an extraordinary&nbsp;
amount of energy and adaptability. So I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:49.707
I think a willingness to sell without fear and&nbsp;
then that high energy. I think that that's —&nbsp;

00:15:49.707 --> 00:15:54.320
Tim Ferriss: All right. So to tie up just&nbsp;
a loose end here and bring this boomerang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.320 --> 00:16:03.600
back to the initial question for a minute. So is&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne to Batman as Peter is to Charlie Tuna?&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:06.584
Andrew Rosener: 100 percent.
Tim Ferriss: They're one and the same?&nbsp;

00:16:06.584 --> 00:16:08.340
Andrew Rosener: One and the same.
Tim Ferriss: All right. So what —&nbsp;

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:14.880
Andrew Rosener: And Charlie Tuna, he was a&nbsp;
squash player. Peter influenced me in ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:23.347
way beyond business. His lifestyle, I had&nbsp;
never seen somebody with that lifestyle.&nbsp;

00:16:23.347 --> 00:16:28.200
Tim Ferriss: What was his lifestyle?
Andrew Rosener: Well, again, he's an old hippie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:32.840
who had been — he was playing the long game. He&nbsp;
had this little business and he loved it, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:39.000
it made a couple million bucks a year every year.&nbsp;
And good times, he made more and bad times, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:44.080
made a million bucks, but it was a great business.&nbsp;
And he'd just been chugging along, chugging along.&nbsp;

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:49.240
He had no ambitions of scaling. I came in,&nbsp;
I'm fresh out of college and business school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:54.440
and I'm like, "Well, we can do this and we can&nbsp;
automate this and we can do a lot." And he's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.440 --> 00:17:02.000
"Look, shut up kid. Just keep chugging." And&nbsp;
he would take off. He'd go down to Belize for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:08.800
a month and he had a chalet on the edge of&nbsp;
a cliff in the middle of nowhere in Gaspé,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:15.040
in way up northern Canada, and he'd just go up&nbsp;
and hang out at this chalet and watch whales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:23.600
break in the surface. And he didn't aspire for&nbsp;
the things — I guess at that time, you look up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:27.960
to these people that you think are successful&nbsp;
and you think, "Wow." And you try to model the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:32.080
way that they live their life or the things that&nbsp;
they buy or mark as trophies of their success.&nbsp;

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:41.960
And Peter didn't have any of that. He optimized&nbsp;
for what I would say is freedom. That's how I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:50.120
sort of digested it into my own life and what&nbsp;
I've modeled. He optimized for freedom. Anyways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:04.560
he got the Charlie Tuna moniker from his&nbsp;
squash — he was a competitive squash player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:08.400
and that was his nickname was Charlie&nbsp;
Tuna. And he got me into squash.&nbsp;

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:15.960
Tim Ferriss: So what are the tenets&nbsp;
of Charlie Tuna, or some of them?&nbsp;

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:24.360
Andrew Rosener: I actually meant to bring it&nbsp;
because these tenets are like mantras to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:32.880
but it's a few sayings that either he identified&nbsp;
or people that he worked with or customers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:38.720
he worked with for a long time identified&nbsp;
as things that he would just say a lot. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:45.000
they're just these short little phrases that get&nbsp;
a certain point across. And he had 10 of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:50.680
they were on the wall in the office. I've&nbsp;
expanded it to about 15. I still say the 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:56.040
tenets of Charlie Tuna because it just sounds&nbsp;
better, but it's like, "I'm not Mother Teresa;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.040 --> 00:19:01.960
I'm here for a profit." Because you get customers&nbsp;
and they're like, "Well, can you do this for me? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:06.840
really, dah, dah." And I think, "Look, I'm here&nbsp;
for business. If I was here to make friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:11.600
we wouldn't be on the phone. We'd be meeting&nbsp;
somewhere for a beer. But I'm not Mother Teresa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:17.460
and I'm here to make a profit." And unabashedly.&nbsp;
Or, "Don't be backwards about going forwards."&nbsp;

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:21.000
Tim Ferriss: What does that mean?
Andrew Rosener: Well, people do things in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:31.270
roundabout ways. Just be direct. Just say what you&nbsp;
want to say. Do what it is you want to do. Don't —&nbsp;

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:32.840
Tim Ferriss: Beat around the bush.
Andrew Rosener: Dance around it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:37.480
Don't beat around the bush. There's one,&nbsp;
which I literally, I've still to this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:41.156
never understood, which was, "Don't crowd the&nbsp;
mourners." I don't know what that one meant.&nbsp;

00:19:41.156 --> 00:19:42.756
Tim Ferriss: The mourners?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah, "Don't crowd the mourners."&nbsp;

00:19:42.756 --> 00:19:47.680
Tim Ferriss: Okay. There's&nbsp;
still some riddles unsolved.&nbsp;

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:50.254
Andrew Rosener: That one I&nbsp;
never got a clear answer to.&nbsp;

00:19:50.254 --> 00:19:52.560
Tim Ferriss: All right. I feel like we were&nbsp;
having sushi at one point and you were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.520
"Something, something in the whorehouse."
Andrew Rosener: Oh, yeah. One of the better ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.520 --> 00:20:02.160
"You can't run a whorehouse without any whores."
Tim Ferriss: All right, on its face, this makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:06.560
sense, but what did that mean in the business?
Andrew Rosener: But in its original context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:14.200
it's like, if we don't have enough inventory,&nbsp;
we can't do business because it's an arbitrage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:19.760
deal. So if I don't have what the customers —&nbsp;
we only have a limited pool of customers. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:27.200
not trying to be Walmart. So if I don't have&nbsp;
what these people want, then I'm just losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:32.160
business. And so if you want to run a scallop&nbsp;
shop, you'd better have scallops. If you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:37.520
to run a domain brokerage, you'd better have the&nbsp;
best domains in stock, right? It makes sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:46.880
But you'd be shocked if you look around and you&nbsp;
examine failures, how often that's the case that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.120
people are trying to run a whorehouse without&nbsp;
any whores, because generally they're trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.640
avoid risk, right? They're trying to de-risk.
Tim Ferriss: I imagine some people listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:57.680
would say, "Well, hold on a second..." I keep&nbsp;
wanting to call him Charlie. I guess that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:01.587
fine. But Peter ran a really —
Andrew Rosener: Tight shop.&nbsp;

00:21:01.587 --> 00:21:07.400
Tim Ferriss: Tight shop. He seemed to carry much&nbsp;
lower overhead and fixed cost because he wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:13.560
virtually integrated like these competitors,&nbsp;
which all makes sense. So they might then extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:18.000
that to say, "Well, if you're running an arbitrage&nbsp;
business, could you not identify the need and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:20.276
source the inventory?" Or are you just too slow?
Andrew Rosener: Too slow.&nbsp;

00:21:20.276 --> 00:21:22.460
Tim Ferriss: You've missed the window?
Andrew Rosener: Missed the window.&nbsp;

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:23.560
Tim Ferriss: Got it.
Andrew Rosener: Miss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:30.320
window. It's a commodity, so you don't have it,&nbsp;
the next call is your competition. Very little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:34.600
differentiation. It's a commoditized product.
Tim Ferriss: So you must have to get pretty — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:40.880
would imagine, to make that work, and maybe I'm&nbsp;
imagining incorrectly, but pretty sophisticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:46.040
with forecasting and prediction and so on,&nbsp;
because scallops, last I checked, don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:49.424
last forever. So if you're holding inventory,&nbsp;
it's not like you can sell the next Christmas.&nbsp;

00:21:49.424 --> 00:21:52.120
Andrew Rosener: 100 percent. I don't know how&nbsp;
deep down this rabbit hole you want to go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:58.720
but that was one of our superpowers was, and&nbsp;
another way that Peter has really influenced me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:08.280
was, Peter had lived all over the world. He dodged&nbsp;
the Vietnam draft and went to Namibia. He's lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:14.520
all over the world and he's traveled all over&nbsp;
the world, literally all over, everywhere. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:19.600
he had these relationships that were just the&nbsp;
most absurd relationships. Everybody had crazy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:24.904
nicknames. In Chile, in Peru, we had —
Tim Ferriss: Pablo the Catch.&nbsp;

00:22:24.904 --> 00:22:27.960
Andrew Rosener: We had Mr.&nbsp;
Lucky in China, and we had —&nbsp;

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:31.800
Tim Ferriss: Mikey the Iron Wrench.
Andrew Rosener: Crazy, really. And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:38.720
just knew these people and he would&nbsp;
call them up and he'd just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:43.160
"What's it looking like in Peru?" "What's&nbsp;
it looking like in China this year?" "What's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:46.840
it looking like in Japan?" "What's it looking&nbsp;
like..." And every morning, that was literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:49.040
the first thing I did every single morning.
Tim Ferriss: And that's in terms of catch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:49.947
type of catch?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:22:49.947 --> 00:22:52.304
Tim Ferriss: Or catch may&nbsp;
not be the right term, but —&nbsp;

00:22:52.304 --> 00:22:58.320
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. A lot of these guys, they&nbsp;
came to appreciate Peter and his personality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:04.280
He's truly a character, and the method. But&nbsp;
I would make these calls, I had a list of 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:09.480
people that I would call every single day. And I&nbsp;
literally wrote out a report of what that person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:14.800
said every single day. And I presented it to Peter&nbsp;
every day. And this isn't a fast-moving business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:18.400
And so generally speaking, whatever they told me&nbsp;
yesterday is the same thing they're telling me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:26.680
today, and so it was redundant, but what it did is&nbsp;
it honed a whole bunch of different skills and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:31.040
caused me to build a relationship with these&nbsp;
people in a way that Peter had built over an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:36.200
extraordinarily long period of time. And so it got&nbsp;
them to trust me, it got them to know who I was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:40.240
and it got them to share information with me&nbsp;
that they weren't sharing with other people.&nbsp;

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:49.840
And most other companies had a more local-centric&nbsp;
focus on supply, so they knew what the catch was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:54.760
like in the North Atlantic, but they didn't know&nbsp;
what the imported supply was going to be from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.760 --> 00:24:02.160
China or from Peru or from Chile where there's&nbsp;
these massive aquaculture operations that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:09.440
happened to be at that time really scaling up to&nbsp;
match or now far surpass the domestic supply. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:16.560
that allowed us to understand where price was&nbsp;
going to go and to understand which part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:24.040
market. So when you say you've got to have the&nbsp;
whores to run the whorehouse, you don't have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:28.960
have all of them. You just need to know that&nbsp;
the guy coming in the door wants a redheaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:35.820
whatever. So I need to know that the thing that's&nbsp;
going to be short in the market is going to be 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:42.320
40 size scallops, 20, 30 size scallops, whatever.&nbsp;
I need to know what's going to be short.&nbsp;

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:48.120
Because the stuff that's going to be abundant,&nbsp;
I can get that readily. I will know the price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:53.480
readily. I won't get caught with my pants down on&nbsp;
the price. And so I can do what you were saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:58.880
before, which is what we call the back-to-back.&nbsp;
But by having the stuff that's in short supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:03.040
I'm going to get the business because&nbsp;
I'm going to have the thing they need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:07.680
even if maybe I'm going to be a nickel or&nbsp;
a dime higher on the other stuff that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:08.290
abundant, I'm going to be the guy that's got&nbsp;
the right price on the stuff they need that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:08.389
can't get from everywhere. So that was really our&nbsp;
superpower, was knowing where to fill the holes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.389 --> 00:25:15.520
which gaps were going to give us that advantage&nbsp;
that would allow us to compete on the stuff we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:21.640
otherwise wouldn't have been able to compete with.
Tim Ferriss: We're going to move from tuna as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:27.680
in the namesake of Batman/Charlie Tuna. We're&nbsp;
going to move from scallops to jamón ibérico.&nbsp;

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:29.720
Andrew Rosener: It's a good segue.
Tim Ferriss: We're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:32.240
to move from food to food.
Andrew Rosener: Okay. It's a good segue.&nbsp;

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:34.424
Tim Ferriss: Where does this&nbsp;
fit into your life story?&nbsp;

00:25:34.424 --> 00:25:40.360
Andrew Rosener: So I did a semester abroad&nbsp;
in Australia where I met my now-wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:50.400
who's German. And while we were dating, playing&nbsp;
international love affair, she took me to Mallorca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:57.000
where I had jamón ibérico, pata negra. There's&nbsp;
many different names for it. The Portuguese would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:04.680
tell you that the pigs are raised in Portugal and&nbsp;
then cured in Spain. But traditionally speaking —&nbsp;

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.480
Tim Ferriss: With people nodding in the room.
Andrew Rosener: Traditionally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:18.520
it's a Spanish ham from the black-footed pig.&nbsp;
And at that time, in the very early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:25.160
it was illegal to import it into the United&nbsp;
States. And when I tasted this with my now-wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:30.320
I was blown away. It was the best meat I'd ever&nbsp;
had in my life. I couldn't believe it. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:35.480
was like, "Why don't we have this in the United&nbsp;
States?" And I was in the seafood import business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.480 --> 00:26:41.160
so I thought, "I'm going to import this to the&nbsp;
United States." Backtrack to my college days, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:47.360
was literally — the mid-'90s, it's the emergence&nbsp;
of the consumer internet. I took a class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:52.200
learned about the internet. I thought, "Wow,&nbsp;
domain names, amazing." I started registering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:57.160
them because I'm hyperactive, and every time&nbsp;
I had had an idea, I'd get a domain for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.160 --> 00:27:03.800
not thinking that these would be valuable.
But anyways, coming back, I thought, "I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:07.240
to get some domains for this jamón ibérico thing,&nbsp;
and I'm going to set up a website and I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:11.680
start importing this stuff." So I got back to the&nbsp;
US, and that was my intention. I discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:17.040
it was actually illegal. The FDA did not allow&nbsp;
you to import this jamón ibérico because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:24.680
cured for 18 months. It's a raw meat that's cured&nbsp;
for 18 months in salt and like French cheese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:31.120
the FDA didn't designate it as fit for human&nbsp;
consumption. So that was that. And I put it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.120 --> 00:27:35.840
in the closet and forgot about it.
And then a couple years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:43.160
I'm driving to my office and I hear on NPR radio&nbsp;
that George Bush is about to get the first jamón&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:50.120
ibérico ever legally imported into the United&nbsp;
States, and I couldn't believe it. And I got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:05.840
his proverbial jaw dropped. He immediately was&nbsp;
like, "How much do you want for those domains?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:11.960
It was the first time — like I said, I bought a&nbsp;
lot of domains at that point already, but never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:16.000
thinking, "These are valuable assets that I'm&nbsp;
going to sell in the future." It was always like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:19.720
"This is another lame-brained idea that I'm going&nbsp;
to try and build a business around that I never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:27.080
got around to." I didn't even know what to answer,&nbsp;
so I said, "I want one of your hams," and he goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:33.160
"No problem, done." I was like, "Wait, that's too&nbsp;
easy," so I was like, "$5,000," and he's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:39.280
"Done." I was like, "The ham's got to come from&nbsp;
that first container, George Bush's container,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:46.507
and he's like, "Done." I was like, "Wow,&nbsp;
okay." I was like, "All right, it's yours."&nbsp;

00:28:46.507 --> 00:28:48.240
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Andrew Rosener: I had a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:54.160
of these domains and I transferred the domains&nbsp;
to him on the spot, and at that time, I had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:59.120
idea how to do a domain transaction. I just took&nbsp;
the leap of faith, transferred him to domains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:08.400
a couple of weeks later, I got a coffin in the&nbsp;
mail and the rest is history. That moment then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:17.320
gave birth to my whole domain business. I realized&nbsp;
that this little esoteric corner of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:23.600
if this guy wanted his domains that bad, every&nbsp;
business in the world is going to want or need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:31.360
their respective domain names. At this point,&nbsp;
I'm still in the seafood business and I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:37.640
making quite a lot of money for somebody in their&nbsp;
mid-20s, and I just started backing up the truck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:43.400
and buying as many domains as I possibly could.
Tim Ferriss: Now, where or when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:48.960
does Tess Diaz into the picture?
Andrew Rosener: Tess Diaz worked for GoDaddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:56.760
and GoDaddy, at this time, again, early 2000s,&nbsp;
they launched what they called "The executive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.760 --> 00:30:00.240
department," or something at that time. They&nbsp;
looked at their accounts and they were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:04.640
"Wow, some of these people have thousands of&nbsp;
domain names, let's reach out to them and figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:09.040
out what they're doing with these domains," so&nbsp;
they set up this little department with a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.040 --> 00:30:13.240
people and their job was to reach out to these&nbsp;
people that had more than a thousand domains and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:17.960
figure out what they were doing with them. Most of&nbsp;
these people knew what they were doing with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:23.760
they had a purpose. I did not. Anyways,&nbsp;
Tess called me and I was actually in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:28.760
car. I was driving up to my good friend JB's&nbsp;
house in upstate New York, Quiet Please Farm.&nbsp;

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:33.200
I had a lot of time, so she called me and she&nbsp;
said, "What are you doing with all these domains?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:38.000
I was like, "I have no idea. Every time I have&nbsp;
an idea, I buy them." She said, "There's a domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:45.520
industry and there's people trading these things&nbsp;
and you can monetize them." I was like, "I had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:50.920
idea, tell me more." Literally, I spent three&nbsp;
or four hours on the phone with her getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:57.480
a download on — that there's a domain industry&nbsp;
and an aftermarket and there's people on forums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.480 --> 00:31:03.600
talking about these things and there's different&nbsp;
ways to park them and monetize them and, "Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:10.840
will pay you for the clicks." This was all new to&nbsp;
me, but it was fascinating. I then spent weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:18.280
as I do, deep diving into this new world of&nbsp;
domain names, and I got super fascinated.&nbsp;

00:31:18.280 --> 00:31:25.120
At this point, I'm married and my wife thinks&nbsp;
I'm crazy. In fact, everybody thinks I'm crazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:28.960
because I've now spent a considerable&nbsp;
amount of money buying domain names.&nbsp;

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:35.000
Tim Ferriss: By that point?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah, by that point. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:39.040
when I got married, my wife said, "Look, I'll&nbsp;
live in the United States for four years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:46.320
and then the shot clock is up and then we're&nbsp;
moving somewhere else." The shot clock ran up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:52.760
and it ran up at the same time that I was doing&nbsp;
this deep dive into this domain world. What I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.760 --> 00:31:59.840
realized was there was nobody playing matchmaker&nbsp;
between the people that had these domains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:04.880
who were fascinating characters. Literally,&nbsp;
you would be hard-pressed to find an industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:13.200
that has such a motley crew of extremely&nbsp;
successful and a variety of standards. People,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:18.200
but from just wildly different backgrounds,&nbsp;
wildly different interests, that have stumbled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:24.560
into this industry in completely different ways.
I decided to basically build a domain brokerage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:28.840
business to play matchmaker. There&nbsp;
was people trading amongst themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:34.680
playing hot potato in the domain industry, but&nbsp;
there was nobody taking these domain names and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:40.760
selling them to the end users that ultimately&nbsp;
could benefit most from them. More importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:50.200
playing educator between this nascent asset class&nbsp;
and the broader business environment that I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:55.880
recognized, "This is an extension of our brand,"&nbsp;
but didn't understand why these things might cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.880 --> 00:33:02.560
so much and why they're so valuable and the&nbsp;
various benefits of owning them. Therein was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:08.280
born Media Options. My wife and I founded it&nbsp;
together, and I think it was maybe a year or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:14.560
two later that Tess — Tess became my account&nbsp;
rep at GoDaddy, just going full circle here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:19.667
and then ultimately, left GoDaddy to come to work&nbsp;
for me. I went from a Lone Ranger to plus one.&nbsp;

00:33:19.667 --> 00:33:23.280
Tim Ferriss: VP of sales.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. Shortly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:29.467
thereafter, we moved to Panama. We&nbsp;
left Rhode Island and moved to Panama.&nbsp;

00:33:29.467 --> 00:33:34.280
Tim Ferriss: If we take a 30,000-foot view for&nbsp;
a second for folks who are unfamiliar with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:42.280
domain/domaining world, and I would put myself&nbsp;
in that category. Maybe I know the edges of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:48.280
puzzle, but I really don't know what the entire&nbsp;
thing looks like at all. There's an aftermarket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:55.360
there are auctions, expiries, dnjournal.com,&nbsp;
dnforum.com, domainnamewire.com, expired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.360 --> 00:34:04.600
auctions. Can you just give us an overview of&nbsp;
what that world looks like? What are the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:11.640
pieces? The main positions, so to speak?
Andrew Rosener: Like any market, it has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:18.000
structure, there's a clear texture and structure&nbsp;
to the market. Let's say, at the lowest level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:24.200
you've got guys that just really appreciate domain&nbsp;
names and they're investing in domain names. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:29.840
they're hoping to strike it rich like a lottery&nbsp;
ticket, maybe they're like me and they just have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:34.800
eccentric ideas and register domains around them.&nbsp;
Maybe they drank too much and thought they had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:38.800
clever idea that they regret in the morning, but&nbsp;
there's just people that are collecting domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:41.400
names, and those are primary registrations.
Then you've got people that are really taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:47.040
it seriously, investing into domain names,&nbsp;
and they've got varying methodologies. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:54.240
got one of my very good friends, Yani, is super&nbsp;
methodical, he's a robot. He's got algorithms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:59.960
There's very little emotion or anything that goes&nbsp;
into his formula, whereas I'm the opposite. I'm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.960 --> 00:35:06.880
basically, 100 percent on instinct and then I back&nbsp;
it up with data, but I primarily am operating from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:15.680
instinct. Either way, we utilize primarily the&nbsp;
same tools as the SEO industry to gauge what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:20.600
the total addressable market of these domains is.
There's a whole bunch of different tool providers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:26.480
in the domain industry to uncover who owns a&nbsp;
domain, trying to evaluate a domain based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:31.080
a bunch of data points. There's a bunch of&nbsp;
different tool providers. Then you've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:36.480
forums where these people meet. Less today,&nbsp;
it's less relevant today, I would say Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:44.160
is the — X. Another segue we can go down, but&nbsp;
X is really where the material domain world is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:48.280
meeting these days. There's investors coming&nbsp;
out at this from a lot of different angles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:53.240
and then you've got some of the names that&nbsp;
you mentioned, Domain Name Wire, DN Journal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:59.640
I would say those are the two most prominent.
They are covering the news, these are our true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:07.000
journalists for the domain name industry,&nbsp;
and they're talking about policy changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:12.920
Domain names are governed by ICANN, which is the&nbsp;
multi-stakeholder, non-governmental organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:18.920
that basically governs domain names. Remarkably&nbsp;
important organization, by the way, that very few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:18.920 --> 00:36:24.280
people know even exists, and more people should&nbsp;
know that they exist. There's all these different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.280 --> 00:36:30.400
layers, and then you've got — as the industry&nbsp;
evolved through the late '90s and then the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:38.280
2000s, these good domain names get, what we call,&nbsp;
type-in traffic. It is just inherent traffic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:43.224
people just inherently go to these domain names.
Tim Ferriss: Prime.com?&nbsp;

00:36:43.224 --> 00:36:47.160
Andrew Rosener: Yeah, prime.com. People want&nbsp;
to go to Prime at Amazon, they just type in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:55.440
prime.com. We helped Amazon to get prime.com for&nbsp;
that very reason, because tens of thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.440 --> 00:37:01.360
people were going to prime.com every day and&nbsp;
not finding Amazon. If you own these domains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:10.800
though, you can park them with Google or Yahoo&nbsp;
on their parking feeds and they display keyword&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:16.600
advertising, and then when people click them, you&nbsp;
earn a couple cents, sometimes more. At scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:24.627
that can be considerable revenue, and that used to&nbsp;
be the primary business model in the early 2000s.&nbsp;

00:37:24.627 --> 00:37:30.600
Tim Ferriss: Let's capture that traffic, hope&nbsp;
it leads to click-throughs on your advertising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:33.307
and then get paid by Google.
Andrew Rosener: 100 percent.&nbsp;

00:37:33.307 --> 00:37:37.920
Tim Ferriss: Just out of curiosity, because I know&nbsp;
I'm not the only person who knows what it is now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.920 --> 00:37:42.800
I'm not giving a real example, but I'm typing&nbsp;
something like "Google" really quickly and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:49.160
type an extra O, right? Google, three Os, and&nbsp;
I land on that page. How much do you think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:55.000
owners of some of these misspellings pull in?
Andrew Rosener: I'm going to pause for a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:59.320
because this is a really important point that I&nbsp;
want to make. I would say, if your audience got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:06.600
one point from me today, I really want it to be&nbsp;
this. There is a very clear distinction between a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:13.560
domain investor and a domain squatter. The guy&nbsp;
that owns Google with three Os is a squatter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:23.560
he is the person, he, she, whatever, is the person&nbsp;
that gives the domain industry a bad rap in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:31.560
late '90s, in the early 2000s. That is worn off&nbsp;
— it still resonates, but it's mostly worn off at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:36.720
this point, but in the early days of my business,&nbsp;
that was the biggest hurdle. Like, "You're just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:43.360
dirty domain squatter." It was a really bad — the&nbsp;
fact that somebody was an investor in domain names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:51.160
was a negative connotation, and funny enough, some&nbsp;
of the biggest domain investors are famous VCs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:56.347
that are funding the companies that are accusing&nbsp;
domain investors of being squatters. Anyways.&nbsp;

00:38:56.347 --> 00:38:59.823
Tim Ferriss: Really. There are VCs who own just —
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:38:59.823 --> 00:39:03.800
Tim Ferriss: — spreadsheets full of domains?
Andrew Rosener: More targeted, super high quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:03.800 --> 00:39:10.120
maybe tens of domains, but really high quality.
Tim Ferriss: Are they holding them for portfolio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:11.920
companies or are they looking to —
Andrew Rosener:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.920 --> 00:39:14.560
I don't know, I don't know.
Tim Ferriss: Can I play stand-in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:16.627
for the listeners for a second?
Andrew Rosener: Sure.&nbsp;

00:39:16.627 --> 00:39:17.200
Tim Ferriss: All right.
Andrew Rosener: Please, do.&nbsp;

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:21.800
Tim Ferriss: This differentiation,&nbsp;
I think, is important to underscore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:31.720
squatter versus investor. Do investors still&nbsp;
buy URLs that represent trademarks owned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:36.040
other people? If so, how is that reconciled?
Andrew Rosener: There's always going to be bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:41.320
actors, but it is a very, very, very small&nbsp;
fraction of what it used to be. We've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:47.920
cybersquatting laws now, it's quite punitive,&nbsp;
$100,000. If you get sued in federal court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:53.440
it's $100,000 per name penalty, for&nbsp;
infringing on somebody's trademarks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:59.280
cybersquatting. There are also much cheaper,&nbsp;
faster, and easier mechanisms for recovering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:06.227
domain names that infringe on your trademark&nbsp;
called the UDRP, Universal Domain Resolution —&nbsp;

00:40:06.227 --> 00:40:07.507
Tim Ferriss: Protocol?
Andrew Rosener: Protocol.&nbsp;

00:40:07.507 --> 00:40:10.320
Tim Ferriss: I'm just guessing.
Andrew Rosener: I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.320 --> 00:40:15.080
"Protocol." Basically, there's three elements,&nbsp;
and if you could prove those three elements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:19.280
the domain's going to immediately get transferred&nbsp;
to you, okay? It's only going to cost you about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:26.040
let's say, $2,500, maybe three grand if you go&nbsp;
for a three-person panel for this arbitration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:31.920
They're going to make a decision, the owner gets&nbsp;
to respond if they choose to, and in most of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:36.600
cases where it's a clear trademark infringement,&nbsp;
they don't even respond. Then that's it, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:39.720
going to get to the domain name assuming they —
Tim Ferriss: You don't need to know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:44.707
identity of the holder for that.
Andrew Rosener: Nope, you don't.&nbsp;

00:40:44.707 --> 00:40:46.560
Tim Ferriss: Got it.
Andrew Rosener: There's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:54.360
strong mechanisms both preventing and, let's&nbsp;
say, clearing the market. There's very little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:54.360 --> 00:41:00.880
upside to doing this anymore. Like I said,&nbsp;
there's still bad actors, but it's a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:07.600
small problem compared to what it used to be.
Tim Ferriss: When did you put up your shingle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:10.387
and start the company?
Andrew Rosener: 2008.&nbsp;

00:41:10.387 --> 00:41:17.704
Tim Ferriss: I'm no historian, but 2008&nbsp;
doesn't sound super early in the domain game.&nbsp;

00:41:17.704 --> 00:41:19.760
Andrew Rosener: No.
Tim Ferriss: Which makes it more interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:24.800
to me, because it's not day one of Google&nbsp;
AdWords where you're shooting fish in a barrel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:28.920
right? There's a point in time where it was like,&nbsp;
"Yeah, we can just look at the dictionary and go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:30.920
buy a bunch of dictionaries."
Andrew Rosener: Totally.&nbsp;

00:41:30.920 --> 00:41:38.160
Tim Ferriss: At this point, it would seem like&nbsp;
you are buying or matching, or both. Maybe you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:43.440
could explain the business model then, what it&nbsp;
looked like, which is incredibly interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:49.400
because it raises the question of how to&nbsp;
value or how to determine a reasonable price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.400 --> 00:41:57.340
acceptable purchase price. I wouldn't know&nbsp;
how to begin doing that for something like X —&nbsp;

00:41:57.340 --> 00:41:57.982
Andrew Rosener: Nor would&nbsp;
almost anybody else like.&nbsp;

00:41:57.982 --> 00:41:59.747
Tim Ferriss: Like x.com, right?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:41:59.747 --> 00:42:07.440
Tim Ferriss: I don't know who would use it for&nbsp;
what. I know it's a single letter, okay. Could you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:14.800
walk us through what the business model was at the&nbsp;
time? You're coming in, clearly not too late, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:21.320
certain low-hanging fruit. Most of the low-hanging&nbsp;
fruit had been, a lot of them, removed, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:22.360 --> 00:42:27.280
Right, so then how do you determine a fair price?
Andrew Rosener: I'm taking a wild guess here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.280 --> 00:42:34.120
but just for emphasis, literally, probably 95, 98&nbsp;
percent of our business is domain names that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.120 --> 00:42:43.040
registered prior to 2000. In 2008, when, as you&nbsp;
said, hung up my shingle and started the business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:49.160
I am super late to the party. Now, I&nbsp;
have started accumulating some domains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:54.960
but most of them weren't really great at that&nbsp;
point, but I understood that it was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.960 --> 00:43:00.400
to be a very capital-intensive business,&nbsp;
and I had already spent a lot of capital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:05.360
and if I wanted to continue this hobby, I&nbsp;
needed to generate cashflow to support it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.360 --> 00:43:12.600
okay? That was one of the premises of starting&nbsp;
Media Options. Then that premise worked, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:18.480
started successfully selling other people's domain&nbsp;
names using our commissions to then fund the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:22.720
acquisition of better domain names for ourselves.
Tim Ferriss: People would come to you and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:27.880
"I have fillintheblank.com&nbsp;
and I would like to sell it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:30.680
but I'm not sure how to do it."
Andrew Rosener: A line out the door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:36.600
One thing that is — there's a handful of domain&nbsp;
investors that understand what they've got and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:36.600 --> 00:43:44.760
they have no real eagerness or intention&nbsp;
to sell except when somebody makes them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:53.120
but everybody else is literally — they buy a&nbsp;
domain and they expect it's going to sell the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:53.120 --> 00:43:59.800
next day. Anybody that can help them achieve that&nbsp;
unrealistic objective, they are banging on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:01.800
door. Yes, I have literally — it could be a segue&nbsp;
into a million things, but let's stay here. I get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:08.800
so many emails every day of people that want me to&nbsp;
sell their domains, so endless supply of inventory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:14.920
to sell. I start banging on doors and, basically,&nbsp;
playing fake it until I make it, because nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:19.120
had really done this before, so I was —
Tim Ferriss: How did people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:19.120 --> 00:44:21.160
find you at that point?
Andrew Rosener: Most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.160 --> 00:44:24.720
people that owned the domains I'm selling&nbsp;
at that point, that's no longer the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:29.840
but at that point, most of the domains that&nbsp;
I'm selling are owned by domain investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.840 --> 00:44:32.384
so I'm meeting these people on the forums.
Tim Ferriss: I see.&nbsp;

00:44:32.384 --> 00:44:37.520
Andrew Rosener: There's DN Journal, which does&nbsp;
this weekly sales report. At that time. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:42.400
I'm the most discreet person you're&nbsp;
ever going to meet, but at that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.400 --> 00:44:46.560
trying to build a business, every time I&nbsp;
make a sale, I would announce that sale.&nbsp;

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:49.880
Tim Ferriss: It would get published on the forums?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. It'd get published in DN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:49.880 --> 00:44:54.200
Journal, that was a source of pride. It was like,&nbsp;
"Look, my sale got published in DN Journal this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:54.200 --> 00:45:01.560
week." People would see that you sold a domain for&nbsp;
a good price and then double down on. That was how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:03.640
the people that owned the domains were finding me.
Tim Ferriss: Were finding you, got it.&nbsp;

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:06.520
Andrew Rosener: I'm finding the people&nbsp;
that I was trying to sell them to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.520 --> 00:45:12.020
okay? Very rarely are they coming and finding&nbsp;
me. Did happen, right? Lightning strikes —&nbsp;

00:45:12.020 --> 00:45:18.680
Tim Ferriss: Do you still remember any of your&nbsp;
initial sales where you're like, "Holy shit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.680 --> 00:45:22.920
I think this could be a thing." Do you remember?
Andrew Rosener: The sale where I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.920 --> 00:45:25.560
"Wow. Okay, I think I'm going&nbsp;
to be able to make this into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.560 --> 00:45:31.800
a business," was pizza.net, which —
Tim Ferriss: This is a great example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:35.240
because it seems like there are a&nbsp;
lot of people you could potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.240 --> 00:45:43.720
sell that to. Walk us through pizza.net then.
Andrew Rosener: Pizza.net was a unique case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.720 --> 00:45:53.920
pizza.net was a very famous domain, because it was&nbsp;
highlighted quite prominently in the movie The Net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.920 --> 00:45:56.744
with Sandra Bullock in 1994.
Tim Ferriss: Really?&nbsp;

00:45:56.744 --> 00:46:06.120
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. This is a 1994 movie and&nbsp;
Sandra Bullock, first time anybody has ever seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.120 --> 00:46:13.320
anybody order a pizza online on the internet.&nbsp;
Sandra Bullock goes to pizza.net and orders a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.320 --> 00:46:24.160
pizza online. Man, it's another rabbit hole, but&nbsp;
pizza.net actually has prior art on a whole bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.160 --> 00:46:33.360
of patents. Google has the patent on, "Here's&nbsp;
your IP, we're going to provide you — you search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:37.160
for pizza, we're going to provide you the pizza&nbsp;
restaurants that are within a certain distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:45.440
around your IP address." They have a patent on&nbsp;
that. Pizza.net was doing that prior to Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:52.880
filing that patent, so pizza.net technically had —
Tim Ferriss: The company that owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:59.920
pizza.net was doing that type of proximity —
Andrew Rosener: Yes. Yes. The company was gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.920 --> 00:47:04.680
but the owner of the domain, which — he's also a&nbsp;
fascinating — that's another fascinating story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:09.160
but I don't recall how he found me.
Andrew Rosener: He was the guy that literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:09.160 --> 00:47:17.880
strung the internet cable up the entire East Coast&nbsp;
of the United States. He literally put in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:25.067
actual hardware that created the first internet&nbsp;
network on the East Coast of the United States.&nbsp;

00:47:25.067 --> 00:47:26.640
Tim Ferriss: Okay.
Andrew Rosener: Adam — I can't remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:29.280
his last name, but Adam something.
Tim Ferriss: We'll put it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:30.680
the show notes.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:34.040
Tim Ferriss: We'll also put the rest of the tenets&nbsp;
of Charlie Tuna in the show notes, for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.040 --> 00:47:39.840
who are wondering where those might be found.
Andrew Rosener: At the time, in the early '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:39.840 --> 00:47:44.960
when he bought this domain — I want to say it was&nbsp;
registered in '92. Mind you, most people didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:44.960 --> 00:47:52.240
even know the internet existed until about '94,&nbsp;
'95. Regular home adoption didn't really happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.240 --> 00:48:01.840
until '96, '97, but at that time, in '92, '94,&nbsp;
.net was actually the preferred — most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:01.840 --> 00:48:08.160
thought .net, because most of the people using the&nbsp;
internet were network providers. These were ISPs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:08.840 --> 00:48:13.800
it was a read-only or write-only. It was mostly&nbsp;
tech, not in the way that we think about it today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:13.800 --> 00:48:20.080
but really hardware tech companies that&nbsp;
were utilizing the internet at that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:27.120
other than just putting up a splash page like&nbsp;
a business card, and they were using .net. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:32.440
had a lot of very, very good .net domain names&nbsp;
at a time when he could have registered any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:38.960
them in .com. There's so many ways to segue this,&nbsp;
but he actually is the guy that — he originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:48.120
registered — you brought up x.com. There's three&nbsp;
one-letter .com domains in existence, okay? That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:53.320
it, three. It's Z, Q, and X, but Adam —
Tim Ferriss: Why are there only three?&nbsp;

00:48:53.320 --> 00:49:00.600
Andrew Rosener: I'll tell you the story.&nbsp;
Adam actually registered all other 23,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:04.920
okay? Numbers aren't allowed, single numbers&nbsp;
are just not allowed for security conflicts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:09.840
Prevented by ICANN from being registered. He&nbsp;
went and registered all the other one-letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:17.920
.com domain names, but because he wasn't using&nbsp;
them, ICANN then decided, "This is a bad idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.920 --> 00:49:22.800
nobody should be able to have these one letters,"&nbsp;
so they pulled back all but the three which they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:29.000
grandfathered in, because Nissan was using z.com&nbsp;
for their 300Z car, which was quite popular at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:36.000
the time. I think Quest Communications&nbsp;
was using Q, and Elon as it were, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:40.960
using X at the time. It was actually originally&nbsp;
owned by a lawyer and Elon bought it from him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:47.480
prior to merging with Peter Thiel for PayPal.
Those three got grandfathered in and the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:47.480 --> 00:49:52.200
got pulled back. I have read quite a bit about&nbsp;
it. I think the way ICANN was thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:57.720
it was like maybe there was an element of&nbsp;
security, they're a socialist organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:57.720 --> 00:50:04.800
so they probably thought it was unfair for any&nbsp;
one company to dominate a letter of the alphabet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:04.800 --> 00:50:08.240
but for a variety of reasons, they —
Tim Ferriss: Clawed them all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.240 --> 00:50:09.987
back except for three.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:50:09.987 --> 00:50:10.947
Tim Ferriss: Okay.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:50:10.947 --> 00:50:16.600
Tim Ferriss: Got it. Pizza.net.
Andrew Rosener: Pizza.net, long story short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:16.600 --> 00:50:24.360
I sold pizza.net, I think it was for like&nbsp;
$120,000 at the time, which used to be a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:24.360 --> 00:50:33.400
of money. I earned probably a $20,000 commission&nbsp;
on that, probably the owner made $100,000 net,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:33.400 --> 00:50:40.560
more or less, and that was a lot of money for me.&nbsp;
I had this mantra. At that time, I had just left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:40.560 --> 00:50:47.840
the job where I was making about three, 400 grand&nbsp;
a year consistently, and I was 26 years old, or 25&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:47.840 --> 00:50:54.520
years old, whatever it was. I was making a lot of&nbsp;
money, way more than any of my peers. I literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:54.520 --> 00:51:00.680
jumped ship, moved to Panama, started this&nbsp;
brokerage business, and I had a limited amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:00.680 --> 00:51:05.240
of savings. I had spent most of it on domain names&nbsp;
and I had a limited amount of savings. And it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:05.240 --> 00:51:12.800
basically just enough for us to last one year, I&nbsp;
had this mantra of every single day, five days a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:21.480
week, every day, I need to make $250 profit. If I&nbsp;
don't make $250, then I am already falling behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:21.480 --> 00:51:28.267
the eight-ball. But if I can make $250 every&nbsp;
day minimum, then my business will survive.&nbsp;

00:51:28.267 --> 00:51:32.560
Tim Ferriss: How did you arrive at that number?
Andrew Rosener: I calculated what my annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:32.560 --> 00:51:38.920
expenses were going to be in totality and&nbsp;
divided by the number of business days. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:43.880
it was really that simple, and this is another&nbsp;
thing, it came from Peter. It was like, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.880 --> 00:51:50.000
keep your expenses low. All my competitors, they&nbsp;
were doing sort of different things, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:50.000 --> 00:51:57.320
were all trying to be very technical about it and&nbsp;
build scalable businesses and lots of people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.320 --> 00:52:08.080
lots of technology and huge overhead. And at that&nbsp;
time, I had basically no overhead. And so anyways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:08.080 --> 00:52:13.160
I was turning along and doing whatever it took.
I would give away a domain name just to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:17.360
$250 that would say it didn't matter. Even if I&nbsp;
knew I was doing something that was going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:17.360 --> 00:52:24.800
harmful to me, literally, I was not going to end&nbsp;
my day until I made $250. And if I didn't make it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:24.800 --> 00:52:31.680
which was very rare, the next day I had to make,&nbsp;
not double but triple. It was just punishment. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:37.400
was like, okay. So anyways, I really stuck&nbsp;
to that and I think that that was a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:37.400 --> 00:52:44.000
powerful discipline for me in that first year.
Tim Ferriss: But in brief, because I know a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:47.520
folks listening are going to wonder, why Panama?
Andrew Rosener: So we originally planned to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:47.520 --> 00:52:53.320
back to Europe and the sky was falling. And so&nbsp;
we thought we really hated being in the US where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:53.320 --> 00:52:58.880
it was really just being inundated with bad&nbsp;
news all the time. And we just thought, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:58.880 --> 00:53:04.640
well, it's going to just be more of the same&nbsp;
in Europe. Germany would've been the obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.640 --> 00:53:11.640
next step. So we kind of said like, "Okay,&nbsp;
we really want to escape that environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:11.640 --> 00:53:16.880
particularly we're going to launch a new business.&nbsp;
I need some positivity. What I don't need is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.880 --> 00:53:21.560
constant negative vibes around me all the time."
The other thing was that this was — we left in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:21.560 --> 00:53:26.960
November, and so it was like a&nbsp;
bitter New England cold winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:34.920
horrible cold. And I remember my wife got stuck.&nbsp;
She had a manual car. She was driving up a hill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:41.840
in Providence, and she got stuck in the snow.
Tim Ferriss: I can imagine the German curses.&nbsp;

00:53:41.840 --> 00:53:46.240
Andrew Rosener: And so anyways, it was&nbsp;
like, "Look, we're doing this business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.240 --> 00:53:50.640
that we can do from anywhere, and so let's&nbsp;
go somewhere warm." And I was like, "Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:55.440
let's go somewhere warm." And so we kind&nbsp;
of explored all these different places.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:55.440 --> 00:54:01.000
We visited a lot of places in the Caribbean and&nbsp;
we'd gone to Central America, and I had been to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:08.520
Panama actually a couple of years earlier because&nbsp;
of a friend of mine from college was Panamanian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.520 --> 00:54:13.200
and invited me down. And so Panama just checked a&nbsp;
lot of boxes. It was on the US dollar. Most of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:13.200 --> 00:54:20.027
business was going to be in US dollars. So that&nbsp;
eliminated the currency friction of volatility.&nbsp;

00:54:20.027 --> 00:54:22.800
Tim Ferriss: Didn't even think of that.
Andrew Rosener: That was big. That was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:27.920
big reason. It was very safe at that time. Panama&nbsp;
was the fastest growing economy in the world from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.920 --> 00:54:33.960
a GDP perspective. I think it was 13 point&nbsp;
a half percent GDP growth in 2008 or 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:33.960 --> 00:54:38.547
whereas China was number two with 11&nbsp;
percent. Right. So huge GDP growth.&nbsp;

00:54:38.547 --> 00:54:41.680
Tim Ferriss: Why was there so much GDP growth?
Andrew Rosener: Well, Panama —&nbsp;

00:54:41.680 --> 00:54:45.480
Tim Ferriss: So banking sector, or no?
Andrew Rosener: Well, yeah. So the banking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.480 --> 00:54:51.960
sector was growing rapidly. The Panama Canal&nbsp;
was expanding, and as global — we were basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:56.560
really, let's say for that first decade of&nbsp;
the 2000s, that was kind of peak globalism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:56.560 --> 00:55:03.160
It was like, "Wow, everybody's doing trade with&nbsp;
everybody." That got sort of short-stopped with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:03.160 --> 00:55:07.600
the financial crisis, but things were booming.
So anyways, Panama ticked all the boxes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:14.600
That was really why we landed on Panama.
Tim Ferriss: So you're in Panama, pizza.net. Holy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:14.600 --> 00:55:17.787
shit, this is going to cover a lot of $250 days.
Andrew Rosener: Totally.&nbsp;

00:55:17.787 --> 00:55:20.720
Tim Ferriss: Right. This could be a business.
Andrew Rosener: It was super important that I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:20.720 --> 00:55:26.880
hyper-focused on earning $250 a day, which you're&nbsp;
going to have a hard time really building a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:26.880 --> 00:55:34.520
business if your focus is on making $250 a day.&nbsp;
But it was critical for that first year to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.520 --> 00:55:41.120
that small goal and be achieving it regularly.&nbsp;
But then when I got my first bigger win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:46.200
it gave me the ability to take a deep breath&nbsp;
and then say, "Okay, I think this business is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:46.200 --> 00:55:54.880
going to work and I can start thinking bigger."&nbsp;
And I lost the discipline of the $250 a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:54.880 --> 00:56:00.360
but I started hunting bigger fish with the&nbsp;
inherent confidence that came with achieving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:00.360 --> 00:56:05.344
this discipline of hitting these small targets.
Tim Ferriss: So, hunting bigger fish.&nbsp;

00:56:05.344 --> 00:56:07.440
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: This then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.440 --> 00:56:14.560
brings us back to the question of valuations&nbsp;
and acceptable cost. So how do you think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:14.560 --> 00:56:20.688
this? Or how should people think about it&nbsp;
is too broad. But how do you think about it?&nbsp;

00:56:20.688 --> 00:56:24.200
Tim Ferriss [PSA to listeners]: Andrew and&nbsp;
I got into the weeds on domain valuation. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:24.200 --> 00:56:30.000
really got into the technicalities, which is super&nbsp;
interesting. So be sure to stick around to hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:37.680
Andrew's answer related to how do you actually&nbsp;
determine an objectively defensible valuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:43.120
for domain names. Fascinating topic, but it got&nbsp;
quite detailed, so we moved it to the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.120 --> 00:56:44.747
conversation. So stick around.
[Conversation resumes.]&nbsp;

00:56:44.747 --> 00:56:49.040
Tim Ferriss: What I wanted to ask about is&nbsp;
something that people might find surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:49.040 --> 00:56:54.680
and this is a note that I have in&nbsp;
front of me. I was surprised too.&nbsp;

00:56:54.680 --> 00:56:58.120
It says: "Startups, in particular, should&nbsp;
not buy their domain name. They should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:02.800
lease the best possible .com domain name&nbsp;
to their business with an optional path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.800 --> 00:57:10.400
to buy later." Can you elaborate on this?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. Yeah. So this is a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.280 --> 00:57:18.520
decision/important decision/terrifying&nbsp;
risk for a lot of startups —&nbsp;

00:57:18.520 --> 00:57:21.840
Tim Ferriss: Why do you say what you just said?
Andrew Rosener: — or they wait and then they get,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:22.520 --> 00:57:26.400
extorted is a strong word, but&nbsp;
done in. They really get bent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:26.400 --> 00:57:28.600
over the barrel because they purchase —
Andrew Rosener: Extorted is not actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.600 --> 00:57:32.440
the worst-case scenario. The worst-case&nbsp;
scenario is that they no longer have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:32.440 --> 00:57:39.080
path to ownership for their exact&nbsp;
brand match .com. That's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:39.080 --> 00:57:43.240
the worst-case scenario. That's a much worse&nbsp;
scenario than we have to pay some exorbitant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:57.200
fee to get our domain name. Now explain why.
So about 2015, okay, 2014-2015, we started seeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:57.200 --> 00:58:02.680
really, like a parabolic rise in the value of&nbsp;
domain names. I don't even know really what to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:02.680 --> 00:58:09.160
attribute it to, but there was a sudden&nbsp;
sort of inflection point where more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:09.160 --> 00:58:15.480
more investors, and when I say investors, I mean&nbsp;
really primarily VCs, as well as startup founders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:15.480 --> 00:58:24.240
I think the startup founders part I understand.
That area, around 2012-2015, was sort of a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:24.240 --> 00:58:30.960
of the guys that had a win, or let's say might&nbsp;
have had a win, but then didn't sell fast enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:30.960 --> 00:58:37.400
when the .com boom happened, but a lot of those&nbsp;
like early .com successes were coming back for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:37.400 --> 00:58:43.920
their second shot in that sort of time period.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's true. I mean I can for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:50.880
sure confirm that, because I was in Silicon Valley&nbsp;
at the time and a lot of guys who had their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:57.560
win in 2007, '08, '09, or maybe that's when they&nbsp;
started a company and they had just taken some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.560 --> 00:59:03.120
secondary off the table, so maybe they had an&nbsp;
IPR, but they were flush with cash and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:03.120 --> 00:59:05.080
looking to build more things.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:10.120
Tim Ferriss: I saw this a lot, 100 percent.
Andrew Rosener: So I think, like anybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:10.120 --> 00:59:15.400
who is successful, they learned from previous&nbsp;
experience, and a lot of them understood that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:15.400 --> 00:59:20.160
okay, I launched on this silly name and I might&nbsp;
have gotten lucky and been successful because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:20.160 --> 00:59:23.920
the market wasn't crowded, but now the market&nbsp;
is crowded and now there's a million people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:23.920 --> 00:59:28.400
that want to compete with me and there's a lot&nbsp;
more resources to fund those people who want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:28.400 --> 00:59:36.280
compete with me, so I need a strategic advantage&nbsp;
that my competitors can't — that gives me a moat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:36.280 --> 00:59:42.880
And a domain name is one of those advantages.
So a lot of repeat founders, this is a trend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:48.760
that I see, first-time founder pushes back a&nbsp;
lot on buying their exact brand match .com. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:48.760 --> 00:59:53.240
see it as a risk, as you said. Most people&nbsp;
perceive it as a risk. What I would tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:53.240 --> 00:59:58.360
is it's actually a de-risking. A lot of —
Tim Ferriss: I want to make sure we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:58.360 --> 00:59:59.720
about leasing.
Andrew Rosener:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.720 --> 01:00:02.387
Yeah. This is why we're going&nbsp;
to circle back to this, okay?&nbsp;

01:00:02.387 --> 01:00:03.200
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Andrew Rosener: Now this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:03.200 --> 01:00:08.840
is actually directly leading into the leasing.
Tim Ferriss: Let me, just to straw man this a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:08.840 --> 01:00:13.600
little bit, if somebody is in a weak&nbsp;
cash position and then it could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:13.600 --> 01:00:20.600
risky to overpay in the early stages —
Andrew Rosener: Sure. But this is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:20.600 --> 01:00:25.040
it's going to dovetail into the leasing. Because I&nbsp;
think regardless of the cash position that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:32.640
in — not regardless completely, but within&nbsp;
a realm, it's a better strategy — leasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:32.640 --> 01:00:39.360
rather than an up-front, just all-out purchase.
So around that time, 2014-2015, domain names&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:39.360 --> 01:00:46.240
really started to rise, and I was sort of telling&nbsp;
you what I attribute that to, but as the values&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:46.240 --> 01:00:52.200
started going up it became harder and harder&nbsp;
to sell these things. There was less people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:52.200 --> 01:00:58.760
who could afford to buy them, and there was some&nbsp;
companies offering financing, but at very high&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.760 --> 01:01:06.400
rates and not super attractive to most businesses.
So I was trying to come up with ways to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:06.400 --> 01:01:14.600
the pool of potential buyers and increase&nbsp;
my ability to close sales. So I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:14.600 --> 01:01:19.080
well, you know, we're all — I say we're all,&nbsp;
because I actually owned a lot of domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:19.080 --> 01:01:24.640
names. We're sitting on these domain names&nbsp;
that we all know are really amazing assets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:24.640 --> 01:01:29.120
they keep going up in value, but they're&nbsp;
so underutilized sitting in our portfolios.&nbsp;

01:01:30.440 --> 01:01:32.720
Really the whole parking thing,&nbsp;
which used to be big business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:32.720 --> 01:01:38.960
like we used to make real money from Google&nbsp;
with parking, but around 2008-2009, it's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:38.960 --> 01:01:45.400
a downhill slope ever since. We used to get the&nbsp;
lion's share of revenue and now we get peanuts.&nbsp;

01:01:45.400 --> 01:01:51.720
So as the values went up sales got harder,&nbsp;
and I came up with this idea of like well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:51.720 --> 01:01:58.680
why don't I just lease you the domain? I'll give&nbsp;
you a fixed price option, so you have an exclusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:58.680 --> 01:02:04.320
option to buy this domain. Nobody else can buy&nbsp;
it. Even if somebody shows up and says, "I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:04.320 --> 01:02:10.200
give $10 million for that domain that you leased&nbsp;
to this guy for a thousand dollars a month,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:10.200 --> 01:02:13.880
nothing I can do about it.
We use escrow.com, is one of our biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:13.880 --> 01:02:19.920
partners, a third-party escrow service. I would&nbsp;
encourage anybody doing a domain transaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:20.440 --> 01:02:25.280
in my opinion the only — maybe if he is a lawyer,&nbsp;
but other than that the only safe way to conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.280 --> 01:02:29.800
a domain transaction. It used to be owned by&nbsp;
Fidelity. Then it was sold to a private guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:29.800 --> 01:02:34.040
and then that guy sold it to Freelancer, which is&nbsp;
a public company from Australia, freelancer.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:34.040 --> 01:02:39.120
who are the sole owner of the company now.
Anyways, we're going to put the domain in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:39.120 --> 01:02:44.600
escrow.com. That way, no matter what somebody&nbsp;
else offers me, no matter if I change my mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:44.600 --> 01:02:51.160
there's nothing I can do. Unless you, as the&nbsp;
person leasing the domain, breaches the contract&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.160 --> 01:02:56.720
that we enter into there's nothing I can do.&nbsp;
You keep making your monthly payments right&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:56.720 --> 01:03:00.627
off into the sunset, you might not even ever&nbsp;
have to talk to me again if you don't want to.&nbsp;

01:03:00.627 --> 01:03:05.520
Tim Ferriss: Quick question. So with escrow.com,&nbsp;
who mediates a dispute where you say —&nbsp;

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:07.920
Andrew Rosener: It goes to arbitration.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. It goes into arbitration?&nbsp;

01:03:07.920 --> 01:03:10.160
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. Third-party arbitration.
Tim Ferriss: And they organize all of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:10.160 --> 01:03:13.720
or is that something you guys organize?
Andrew Rosener: I mean I am their number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:13.720 --> 01:03:22.480
one client. I've done hundreds and hundreds of&nbsp;
millions of dollars in sales and, knock on wood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:22.480 --> 01:03:26.080
I've never one time ever had a&nbsp;
transaction go to arbitration.&nbsp;

01:03:26.080 --> 01:03:30.720
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I'm not saying — if it were —
Andrew Rosener: If it were, it goes through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.720 --> 01:03:36.440
third-party forum, arbitration forum.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Okay. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:36.440 --> 01:03:39.160
will basically organize that?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:39.160 --> 01:03:44.440
they're the ones holding the domain. They've&nbsp;
got an interest in sorting this out as quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.440 --> 01:03:51.160
and amicably as possible. So they hold the&nbsp;
domain. There's usually a payment up front&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.160 --> 01:03:55.680
which basically buys you an option, which like&nbsp;
in the world of finance like any other option.&nbsp;

01:03:55.680 --> 01:04:01.360
You have an option to purchase this domain at&nbsp;
a fixed price for a fixed period of time. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:01.360 --> 01:04:05.920
generally default to five years. I think&nbsp;
that's a great runway. Most businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:05.920 --> 01:04:11.960
at a period of five years, are either going to&nbsp;
make it or break it. It's going to be binary.&nbsp;

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:16.640
At the five-year point either they know&nbsp;
they've got a business and their raising&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:16.640 --> 01:04:22.160
either more funds, or their profitable, or&nbsp;
they achieve some level of scale, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:22.160 --> 01:04:28.360
either have a business or they don't. So at that&nbsp;
five-year point they have — and they can execute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:28.360 --> 01:04:32.320
at any time during the lease. They can execute&nbsp;
after the first monthly payment if they want to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.320 --> 01:04:39.080
but within that five-year period they have a&nbsp;
fixed-price option to buy the domain exclusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:39.080 --> 01:04:46.440
to them. And then they have a monthly lease.
So generally, rough back-of-the-napkin math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:47.520 --> 01:04:54.680
the way these things usually fall is like&nbsp;
somewhere around five percent, it could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:54.680 --> 01:05:00.440
10 percent, the bigger the value usually the&nbsp;
numbers come down, the lower the percentage.&nbsp;

01:05:00.440 --> 01:05:03.824
Tim Ferriss: So it's over that&nbsp;
five-year period, you mean?&nbsp;

01:05:03.824 --> 01:05:09.480
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. So the purchase option —&nbsp;
and it's important that the startup have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.480 --> 01:05:10.840
skin in the game —
Tim Ferriss: Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:10.840 --> 01:05:14.560
I see. That's the initial option cost?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. The initial option —&nbsp;

01:05:14.560 --> 01:05:16.713
Tim Ferriss: Not the total&nbsp;
cost during the five-year —&nbsp;

01:05:16.713 --> 01:05:21.000
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. It's going to be the&nbsp;
initial option. Get some skin in the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:21.000 --> 01:05:24.840
buy yourself an option, and then you've got&nbsp;
a lease component. And the lease component&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:30.840
is going to usually be like a half a point.
Tim Ferriss: So does that option-up-front payment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:30.840 --> 01:05:36.680
act as an advance in the case that you purchase?
Andrew Rosener: In most cases, yes. Generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:36.680 --> 01:05:43.240
speaking, we apply that option fee, so let's say&nbsp;
that you approach me, you want to buy a domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:43.240 --> 01:05:47.600
that domain is $100 thousand, we agree&nbsp;
to one of these lease deals. Maybe you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:47.600 --> 01:05:53.200
going to put five or $10 thousand down up&nbsp;
front, and then you're going to have a 90&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:53.200 --> 01:06:00.240
or $95 thousand fixed price option to buy.
Then you're going to have a lease component,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.240 --> 01:06:07.640
and the lease component is a true lease. Payments&nbsp;
don't go towards the purchase price, but it's half&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.640 --> 01:06:13.480
a point. So like if it's a $100 thousand domain,&nbsp;
you might be paying $500, or maybe $1,000 a month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:14.160 --> 01:06:20.040
It's a half a point or a point a month, which is&nbsp;
going to get the owner of the domain a reasonable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:20.040 --> 01:06:25.880
conservative rate of return of five to 12 percent&nbsp;
rate of return, which is going to account for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:25.880 --> 01:06:31.320
inflation and account for the risk, and you're&nbsp;
going to have the opportunity for less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:31.320 --> 01:06:37.560
the cost to keep your floor clean to operate&nbsp;
on your exact brand match .com from day one.&nbsp;

01:06:37.560 --> 01:06:43.160
So that's the first element if de-risking, is&nbsp;
that, number one, you're never going to have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.160 --> 01:06:51.720
rebrand, which is a very — because of SEO, because&nbsp;
of technical challenges, because of word-of-mouth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:51.720 --> 01:06:57.800
and a whole bunch of different things, a re-brand&nbsp;
while you're at stride is very difficult to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:57.800 --> 01:07:04.680
off and it leads to all sorts of messiness.
Generally speaking, it's going to cost you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:04.680 --> 01:07:08.480
somewhere between, depending on the&nbsp;
complexity of your organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:08.480 --> 01:07:14.720
between 10 and $40 thousand per employee to do a&nbsp;
domain name re-brand, to move from one domain to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:14.720 --> 01:07:20.040
another. Particularly it's higher and higher and&nbsp;
higher the later stage you are in the organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:20.040 --> 01:07:24.400
and the more complexity you have.
So you are leasing the domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:24.400 --> 01:07:30.120
you're operating right from the get-go, you&nbsp;
look like you're a serious business right from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:30.120 --> 01:07:37.520
the get-go. You're not try this or get that.
Tim Ferriss: So a question to you on the sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:37.520 --> 01:07:42.240
side, because now I'm super interested in&nbsp;
this. Now I'm thinking are there people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:42.240 --> 01:07:46.520
just have a stable of domains and they treat it&nbsp;
almost like — and this isn't a perfect analogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.520 --> 01:07:50.480
but like a fixed income portfolio, and they're&nbsp;
like, "I'm not in the domain-selling business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.480 --> 01:07:53.680
I'm actually in the domain-leasing business."
Andrew Rosener: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:07:54.320 --> 01:07:59.560
Tim Ferriss: To run the math on that&nbsp;
model, if you were in that position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:59.560 --> 01:08:03.920
you would want to know what percentage — just&nbsp;
so you can forecast properly, you'd want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:03.920 --> 01:08:10.624
know roughly what percentage you would expect&nbsp;
to convert to buyers at the five-year point.

01:08:10.624 --> 01:08:15.120
Andrew Rosener: Do you care, though? Really?
Tim Ferriss: I'm curious what it is.&nbsp;

01:08:15.120 --> 01:08:22.320
Andrew Rosener: This is literally one of my&nbsp;
favorite topics. This was part of the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:22.320 --> 01:08:28.280
revelation of creating this, was I was sitting&nbsp;
back and I was saying to myself I've got about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:28.280 --> 01:08:35.680
5,000 really good domain names. Let's just take&nbsp;
the top 500. These would all be domain names that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:35.680 --> 01:08:40.800
like, at an absolute minimum, you're talking&nbsp;
about $100 thousand, and that would be cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:40.800 --> 01:08:46.720
so $100 thousand and up. Some of these are eight&nbsp;
figures. Some of these are seven figures. Some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:46.720 --> 01:08:53.480
these are mid-six figures, like $100 thousand&nbsp;
to many millions of dollars, okay, in value.&nbsp;

01:08:53.480 --> 01:08:59.280
The price I would expect to sell them for, if I&nbsp;
just took the top 500 domain names and I — let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:59.280 --> 01:09:07.760
just say I'm giving them away. I'm going to give&nbsp;
away all of them for 500 bucks a month. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.760 --> 01:09:16.800
$250 thousand a month in totally passive income.&nbsp;
And when I say passive income, like you would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:16.800 --> 01:09:24.520
hard-pressed to find more passive income, because&nbsp;
it's not like a real estate portfolio, where once&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:24.520 --> 01:09:26.271
in a while you've got a pissed-off tenant.
Tim Ferriss: There's a dead rat in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:26.271 --> 01:09:27.840
HVAC — you've got to fucking deal with it.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. You've got to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:27.840 --> 01:09:34.360
broken pipes and electricians. Then you've got&nbsp;
local and state real estate taxes. Then you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:34.360 --> 01:09:40.760
got dah, dah, dah, dah, dah, dah. What you get&nbsp;
gross is very different than what you get net,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:41.320 --> 01:09:46.240
and not to mention the time investment.&nbsp;
So I laugh when I hear people in the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:46.240 --> 01:09:52.680
estate business talking about passive income.
But it truly is passive income. It's literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:52.680 --> 01:09:55.440
set it and forget it. I literally&nbsp;
hand the domain off to escrow.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:56.920 --> 01:10:02.200
the servers get pointed to the person leasing&nbsp;
it, and I get a notification once a month that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:02.200 --> 01:10:08.040
the payment was received, end of story. That's it.
So that's a pretty good gig. When I did that math&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:08.040 --> 01:10:13.960
and I was like, "Wow, I like those numbers,"&nbsp;
it got me pretty excited and I started to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:13.960 --> 01:10:19.600
pursue this. I talked to GoDaddy and I talked to&nbsp;
some other players in the industry, saying, "Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:19.600 --> 01:10:26.320
don't we promote this as the way that companies&nbsp;
should sell domain names or acquire a domain?"&nbsp;

01:10:27.600 --> 01:10:35.960
From there the concept evolved in my head to&nbsp;
the point where it's really a no-brainer. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:35.960 --> 01:10:44.000
even if you have the money, like, why lay out&nbsp;
the cash if you can lease it? Even if you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:44.000 --> 01:10:49.520
you want to buy it and you have the money to buy&nbsp;
it, you probably have a better use case for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:49.520 --> 01:10:57.280
cash flow. It's literally going to cost you in&nbsp;
most cases less than you pay to keep your floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:57.280 --> 01:11:03.920
clean in your office to have this domain name.
Like the lowest-paid person in your company costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.920 --> 01:11:10.080
more than your company's branded domain&nbsp;
name per month, so it's a no-brainer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:10.080 --> 01:11:14.560
in my opinion, for almost everybody.
When you go to raise funds and you go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.560 --> 01:11:20.040
your VC, I am, I don't know — IAmVida.com.&nbsp;
I'm an investor in a company called Vida,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:22.600 --> 01:11:27.120
which is a good segue into another element&nbsp;
of this that you sort of referenced.&nbsp;

01:11:27.120 --> 01:11:30.200
But, anyways, it's a lot easier&nbsp;
to raise capital going and saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.200 --> 01:11:36.600
"We are Vida and we have vida.com," rather&nbsp;
than, "We're Try Vida," or "We're vida.io,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:36.600 --> 01:11:43.080
or "We're vida.xyz." You're not a serious&nbsp;
company. It's like you're on training wheels.&nbsp;

01:11:43.800 --> 01:11:49.400
So at some point, and most of the smart VCs&nbsp;
know this, at some point you're going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:49.400 --> 01:11:53.600
to upgrade, because there's a ceiling — you'd be&nbsp;
hard-pressed — there are a few exceptions, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:53.600 --> 01:11:59.120
think you'd be hard-pressed to tell me a company&nbsp;
valued over $1 billion that isn't on a .com. You'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:59.120 --> 01:12:05.837
be pretty hard-pressed. I think there's only&nbsp;
one Fortune 1000 company that is not on a .com.&nbsp;

01:12:05.837 --> 01:12:11.680
So at some point, for a variety of reasons,&nbsp;
you're going to hit a ceiling if you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.680 --> 01:12:16.840
not have your exact brand match .com.
Maybe it's because you want to go public&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.840 --> 01:12:22.560
and your investment bank, which was the case&nbsp;
with Facebook — when they wanted to go public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:22.560 --> 01:12:27.840
the investment bank, whoever it was, the&nbsp;
auditors, whoever it was, came in and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.840 --> 01:12:33.920
"You guys need to own all your IP before we're&nbsp;
going to take you public." So last minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:33.920 --> 01:12:39.880
they had to go out and buy fb.com from American&nbsp;
Farm Bureau, and they paid $8.5 million for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:39.880 --> 01:12:44.200
because literally a banker told them they&nbsp;
can't go public until they wrap up this ID.&nbsp;

01:12:44.720 --> 01:12:49.680
Or maybe you're going to raise your next&nbsp;
round of funding and your VC says, "Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.680 --> 01:12:56.320
if you don't have your .com at this stage, it's&nbsp;
this huge risk. We're looking for 100X here. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:56.320 --> 01:13:00.760
want you to be a $10 billion company or $100&nbsp;
billion company, and you can't do that without&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:00.760 --> 01:13:06.760
your .com. So if you don't get it at this stage,&nbsp;
then we don't know what it's going to cost later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.760 --> 01:13:11.040
or you might not be able to get it later," and&nbsp;
they realize that that's just an inherent risk.&nbsp;

01:13:11.040 --> 01:13:17.600
So there's a bunch of advantages. Let's say&nbsp;
lubricating the fundraising process, de-risking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.600 --> 01:13:25.440
that process in the mind of the investors, as well&nbsp;
as the downsize risk of not doing so and looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:25.440 --> 01:13:32.840
like you're not serious, or you have a short-term&nbsp;
vision, or you're not committed to your brand.&nbsp;

01:13:32.840 --> 01:13:38.480
Tim Ferriss: Vida — you mentioned you were an&nbsp;
investor, and you said that relates to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:38.480 --> 01:13:42.680
element of this. What is that other element?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. So I mentioned that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.680 --> 01:13:50.400
because — so Stephanie Tilenius, who was a&nbsp;
very early employee at Yahoo and PayPal, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:50.400 --> 01:13:55.800
think in 2012, Woman of the Year in Technology,&nbsp;
phenomenal founder, she approached me to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:55.800 --> 01:13:59.803
vida.com. She didn't want to pay —
Tim Ferriss: V-I-T-A?&nbsp;

01:13:59.803 --> 01:14:00.664
Andrew Rosener: V-I-D-A.
Tim Ferriss: Okay. V-I-D-A?&nbsp;

01:14:00.664 --> 01:14:02.880
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. The Spanish word for life.
Tim Ferriss: Yep. Got it.&nbsp;

01:14:02.880 --> 01:14:07.400
Andrew Rosener: She was building this health&nbsp;
tech business and she wanted to buy vida.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:07.400 --> 01:14:11.480
and she understood — I mean she had&nbsp;
worked for and had worked with a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:11.480 --> 01:14:16.720
of Silicon Valley's most famous founders,&nbsp;
and she was committed to owning vida.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:16.720 --> 01:14:20.320
and she didn't want to pay the&nbsp;
price. I won't get into the numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:20.320 --> 01:14:25.080
but she didn't want to pay my price. I loved&nbsp;
that. That was one of my favorite domain names.&nbsp;

01:14:25.080 --> 01:14:33.080
Tim Ferriss: You say that to all the&nbsp;
girls, "This one is my favorite."&nbsp;

01:14:34.280 --> 01:14:41.200
Andrew Rosener: So we were working through&nbsp;
ideas on how could this work, and if anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.200 --> 01:14:47.000
I'm extremely open-minded. So I was just throwing&nbsp;
spaghetti into the wall to see what would stick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.000 --> 01:14:51.760
and I was like, "Look, give me a piece of&nbsp;
equity in lieu of cash." She was saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.760 --> 01:14:56.040
"Oh, that's an idea." So she went back to&nbsp;
her board and came back and we did a deal.&nbsp;

01:14:56.040 --> 01:15:02.400
There was some cash, I think there was milestone&nbsp;
cash payments, and there was a small piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.400 --> 01:15:08.480
equity valued at precisely the delta. There was&nbsp;
no funny business. I didn't increase the price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:08.480 --> 01:15:11.800
It was just we're going to make up for that&nbsp;
delta between what we're going to pay in cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:11.800 --> 01:15:16.480
and what you wanted in cash with equity.
Tim Ferriss: When you say milestone-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:16.480 --> 01:15:19.760
cash payments, what type of milestones? Or&nbsp;
hypothetically? Was it like "We raise this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:19.760 --> 01:15:22.780
round and we give you a little more, then we&nbsp;
raise this round and give you a little more?"&nbsp;

01:15:22.780 --> 01:15:25.000
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. Or&nbsp;
even just as simple as time.&nbsp;

01:15:25.000 --> 01:15:31.680
Tim Ferriss: Got it. Your payment schedule?
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. And that deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:32.680 --> 01:15:40.120
like the leasing idea, triggered in my mind that&nbsp;
I can actually act like a venture capitalist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:40.120 --> 01:15:46.040
But instead of putting capital money into&nbsp;
these companies, I can treat these domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:46.040 --> 01:15:54.920
names as capital, and interestingly I get the best&nbsp;
position on the cap table because in my contracts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:55.600 --> 01:16:00.827
if you fail, I just claw the domain back,&nbsp;
because your equity goes to zero. So that was —&nbsp;

01:16:00.827 --> 01:16:05.360
Tim Ferriss: That's an important clause.
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. So it doesn't always work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:05.360 --> 01:16:11.360
right? I mean it depends on who the company is,&nbsp;
who its founders are. It's a negotiation like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:11.360 --> 01:16:18.600
anything else. But generally speaking we've got a&nbsp;
clawback clause. Otherwise we need to mark up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:18.600 --> 01:16:23.280
domain to account for the risk. But we started&nbsp;
doing that, and so now, I don't know, we got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:23.280 --> 01:16:30.120
quite a number of startups that we have a piece&nbsp;
of equity in, that we've literally invested in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:30.120 --> 01:16:35.600
but we didn't invest cash. We invested a domain&nbsp;
name. In some of them I invested cash on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:35.600 --> 01:16:39.240
because I was excited about the opportunity, but —
Tim Ferriss: I thought about doing this with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:39.240 --> 01:16:45.080
podcast occasionally, just because in&nbsp;
a sense I do enjoy the startup game at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:45.080 --> 01:16:50.880
times. I don't want to do it full-time. I&nbsp;
don't want at this point to have a fund or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:50.880 --> 01:16:56.760
anything like that. I just don't want LPs to&nbsp;
deal with, any of that babysitting, frankly.&nbsp;

01:16:56.760 --> 01:17:05.080
But if I have, as you do for instance, a&nbsp;
competitive advantage in spotting things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:05.080 --> 01:17:09.600
that could become very interesting. So&nbsp;
when someone comes in and you learn about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:09.600 --> 01:17:14.800
a company that perhaps hasn't launched, or&nbsp;
hasn't made that quantum leap but they're on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.800 --> 01:17:20.760
the cusp of having bankroll to potentially buy&nbsp;
something, where you have a conversation and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:20.760 --> 01:17:25.920
realize they're very strategically minded,&nbsp;
analytical, like this founder you mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:27.680 --> 01:17:31.542
especially with that clawback, that's interesting.
Andrew Rosener: That's super interesting.&nbsp;

01:17:31.542 --> 01:17:34.520
Tim Ferriss: That diversified business model.
Andrew Rosener: And quite literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:34.520 --> 01:17:39.160
it just interests me. It's a lot more interesting&nbsp;
than a domain sale. It's like, oh, I get to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:39.160 --> 01:17:46.680
along for the ride. So this comes back to another&nbsp;
thing, which is I actually fundamentally believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:46.680 --> 01:17:52.560
that we are still in the early stages of&nbsp;
domain names and domain value, the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:52.560 --> 01:18:00.240
stages of digital commerce and the internet.
Most people in the world have been online for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:00.240 --> 01:18:07.000
less than 25 years. That's a pretty short period&nbsp;
of time. As we see commercial real estate sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:07.000 --> 01:18:12.800
dying and all the distress in commercial real&nbsp;
