Andrew Rosener: In the game for a long time and&nbsp;
have a pretty deep understanding. So if presented&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.440 --> 01:47:48.080
with two choices of this, here's the internet you&nbsp;
know and love. Here's this totally decentralized&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.080 --> 01:47:53.560
new internet that has all these fancy bells and&nbsp;
whistles that you should love, except that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.560 --> 01:48:00.640
this one flaw and that flaw is somebody can put&nbsp;
child porn and there's nobody that can ever take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:00.640 --> 01:48:08.480
it down. And on that singular point is when I&nbsp;
made up my mind that that will never happen. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:08.480 --> 01:48:12.600
Department of Justice, Department of Defense,&nbsp;
which owns the Department of Commerce, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:12.600 --> 01:48:19.907
controls the internet, a lot of people don't know&nbsp;
that, is never, ever, ever, ever going to allow —&nbsp;

01:48:19.907 --> 01:48:23.360
Tim Ferriss: That's wild. I had no idea.
Andrew Rosener: — any browser to resolve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:23.360 --> 01:48:29.240
these things. So you've got some fringe browsers&nbsp;
that do resolve them, but I think it's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:29.240 --> 01:48:33.480
like 98 percent of all the internet traffic passes&nbsp;
through four browsers, right? It's like Firefox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:33.480 --> 01:48:36.504
Safari, Chrome, and what was the —
Tim Ferriss: Explorer?&nbsp;

01:48:36.504 --> 01:48:40.360
Andrew Rosener: Explorer, which is probably&nbsp;
a very small market share these days.&nbsp;

01:48:40.360 --> 01:48:46.240
But that's basically all the traffic. And I can&nbsp;
promise you, I would put virtually everything I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:46.240 --> 01:48:54.320
have on the fact that those browsers are never&nbsp;
going to resolve a Web3 domain name ever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:54.320 --> 01:49:01.480
Primarily for those reasons and other surrounding&nbsp;
reasons like that, the inability to ever censor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:01.480 --> 01:49:08.480
content, as well as the fact that they then&nbsp;
lose control to sniff every packet of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:08.480 --> 01:49:13.960
that passes through the internet. And so that's&nbsp;
never going to happen. It's not going to happen.&nbsp;

01:49:13.960 --> 01:49:20.760
There may be some parallel, like we've got&nbsp;
dark web now, but this is never going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:20.760 --> 01:49:26.520
a mainstream thing ever. But I do think they&nbsp;
make great wallet addresses to some extent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:26.520 --> 01:49:30.960
today. I think that it could get messy.&nbsp;
So you have to be careful. But it's funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:30.960 --> 01:49:34.920
the Ethereum name service guys actually are the&nbsp;
ones that figured out how to do this. So they kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:34.920 --> 01:49:41.040
of shot themselves in the foot. But you can now&nbsp;
take a .com domain name or a .xyz domain name or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:41.040 --> 01:49:48.280
a .net domain name, and you can use the DNS, the&nbsp;
legacy DNS settings, and you can literally make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:48.280 --> 01:49:55.615
your domain name a wallet. And so that, to me is,&nbsp;
it's like that's obvious where we're going to go.&nbsp;

