Last question. The pursuit of happiness. So how&nbsp;
do you think about the pursuit of happiness?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:54.400 --> 01:51:00.080
Because I will say having spent time with you, you&nbsp;
strike me as overall, a pretty happy guy. Yo'veu&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:00.080 --> 01:51:06.184
got a little methy edge to you, A little twitchy,&nbsp;
a little bouncy, but you smile a lot of the time.&nbsp;

01:51:06.184 --> 01:51:06.800
Andrew Rosener: Yep.
Tim Ferriss: I don't know if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:06.800 --> 01:51:10.784
crying on the inside, but you seem like&nbsp;
generally a pretty happy-go-lucky guy.&nbsp;

01:51:10.784 --> 01:51:12.864
Andrew Rosener: I'm a pretty content person.
Tim Ferriss: Content.&nbsp;

01:51:12.864 --> 01:51:15.600
Andrew Rosener: Content. I have&nbsp;
moments of happiness, but I'm pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:15.600 --> 01:51:16.320
content, I think is the right —
Tim Ferriss: Okay. So tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:16.320 --> 01:51:16.840
me more about this and what you —
Andrew Rosener: So I guess the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:16.840 --> 01:51:22.640
I think about it is that I generally&nbsp;
want to be content. I don't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:22.640 --> 01:51:28.280
pursue happiness, I pursue contentment.
Tim Ferriss: Is that wanting what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:28.280 --> 01:51:33.400
have or being grateful? What does that mean?
Andrew Rosener: Yes. I think that's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:33.400 --> 01:51:43.080
way of saying it, but for me personally, it's a&nbsp;
bit more that Terence McKenna had this theory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:43.080 --> 01:51:48.560
novelty that ultimately, that's what evolution&nbsp;
is all about. It's just about the pursuit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:48.560 --> 01:51:55.080
novelty. And that resonated with me. And I&nbsp;
basically surmised that I think the purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:55.080 --> 01:52:01.000
for each of our individual lives is truly, if&nbsp;
you zoom out and you look at it from a species&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:01.720 --> 01:52:06.600
focus as opposed to an individual focus, the&nbsp;
purpose for each individual life is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:06.600 --> 01:52:12.600
just novelty. It's about unique characteristics&nbsp;
that make you you and how you engage with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:12.600 --> 01:52:22.920
world around you and what that leads to. And this&nbsp;
novelty is actually the objective or should be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:22.920 --> 01:52:28.600
objective, again, in my opinion. And so —
Tim Ferriss: Meaning there's a variety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:28.600 --> 01:52:34.338
different characteristics, survival of&nbsp;
the fittest, is that how the novelty —&nbsp;

01:52:34.338 --> 01:52:37.880
Andrew Rosener: Sure. Not necessarily. I think&nbsp;
survival of the fittest is a mechanic in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:37.880 --> 01:52:41.424
game, but it's not necessarily —
Tim Ferriss: The end all be all.&nbsp;

01:52:41.424 --> 01:52:45.120
Andrew Rosener: The end all be all of the game.&nbsp;
I think it's really just about the pursuit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:45.120 --> 01:52:51.120
novelty, that you need to do things that nobody's&nbsp;
done before. You need to react to things in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:51.120 --> 01:52:57.320
way that is not typical. If you do things like&nbsp;
everybody else has done things or everybody else&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:57.320 --> 01:53:01.680
does things, then you can't expect a different&nbsp;
outcome than what everybody else has had. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:01.680 --> 01:53:07.320
I certainly am not looking for the outcome that&nbsp;
everybody else has. It's just not, I got one shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:07.320 --> 01:53:13.560
at this thing, I want to do something else. I want&nbsp;
to stand out. I want to pursue greatness, whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:13.560 --> 01:53:20.000
that means. But I certainly, above all else, I&nbsp;
don't want to be like other people. I don't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:20.000 --> 01:53:24.600
to be like anybody else, like any other individual&nbsp;
or any other group of individuals. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:24.600 --> 01:53:31.480
like labels. I just want to pursue novelty.
And what I found that to mean, once you go another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:31.480 --> 01:53:43.560
layer, is that really what most of life is, is&nbsp;
actually friction and pain and suffering. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:43.560 --> 01:53:50.840
have developed our society to run away from that.&nbsp;
We're always, "You have a right to happiness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:50.840 --> 01:53:57.320
you should be in the pursuit of happiness." And&nbsp;
I think that makes us soft. I think that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:57.320 --> 01:54:07.720
us avoid risk. I think that makes us avoid pain.&nbsp;
It makes us avoid hard work. And everything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:07.720 --> 01:54:15.640
I've seen, everything that I enjoy, comes at the&nbsp;
expense of pain, suffering, hard work, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:15.640 --> 01:54:23.480
that's my marriage, it's a lot of work. It's a&nbsp;
lot of pain and compromise. But through that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:23.480 --> 01:54:32.880
you achieve love and you achieve this amazing&nbsp;
relationship that's irreplaceable. Through those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:32.880 --> 01:54:37.360
days of suffering and doing whatever it takes to&nbsp;
make $250 a day, I was able to build a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:37.360 --> 01:54:45.440
that makes a lot of money and fits my lifestyle.
I've optimized it for exactly the way I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:45.440 --> 01:54:50.760
live. I don't want to build, I don't want to&nbsp;
scale it. I don't want to have 60 employees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:50.760 --> 01:54:56.640
I want to have four, five. I want to just keep&nbsp;
doing what I'm doing, just hone my skill and hone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:56.640 --> 01:55:03.320
my skill and just, I love what I do, but all of&nbsp;
that takes suffering and pain. I think the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:03.320 --> 01:55:15.040
way to highlight it is art. Show me one meaningful&nbsp;
piece of art, cultural art that came as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:15.040 --> 01:55:22.480
of rainbows and butterflies and happiness, right.&nbsp;
Great art comes from pain, suffering, heartache,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:22.480 --> 01:55:31.200
mental illness, just terrible circumstances. Great&nbsp;
art comes from the darkness, not from the light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:31.200 --> 01:55:37.800
but you need both. And if you ignore the darkness,&nbsp;
you will never get the light or you'll always be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:37.800 --> 01:55:45.440
chasing the light. The light will always be in&nbsp;
the distance, but you have to lean into the pain.&nbsp;

01:55:45.440 --> 01:55:50.200
You have to — off camera, we were talking&nbsp;
about something else and you were saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:50.200 --> 01:55:56.560
I had to sit with it. And that's precisely it.&nbsp;
You have to sit with the pain. Don't block it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:56.560 --> 01:56:02.920
out. Don't ignore it, don't push it away. Run&nbsp;
into it. Run into the pain, run into the hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:02.920 --> 01:56:06.880
stuff. Run into the stuff that nobody else wants&nbsp;
to do, because that's how you achieve novelty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:06.880 --> 01:56:12.800
and through novelty, you achieve everything.
Again, anybody, it doesn't matter what your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:12.800 --> 01:56:18.400
definition of success is, I can assure you that&nbsp;
that person is successful by whatever definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:18.920 --> 01:56:24.160
you're holding them up to be, through novelty.&nbsp;
They did something that other people were afraid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:24.160 --> 01:56:31.640
to do, that other people didn't think of. Whatever&nbsp;
it is, it was achieved through novelty. And as far&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:31.640 --> 01:56:36.480
as I can tell, all greatness is achieved&nbsp;
through novelty. It's doing things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:36.480 --> 01:56:40.560
other people don't want to do, doing things that&nbsp;
other people are afraid to do, doing things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:40.560 --> 01:56:48.800
other people wouldn't even think about doing. And&nbsp;
normally the delta between those things is pain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:48.800 --> 01:56:53.840
suffering, heartache. It's the darkness —
Tim Ferriss: Which could take a lot of forms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:53.840 --> 01:56:56.704
right? Like, could take the&nbsp;
form of being ridiculed.&nbsp;

01:56:56.704 --> 01:56:58.264
Andrew Rosener: Absolutely.
Tim Ferriss: Right? As an example.&nbsp;

01:56:58.264 --> 01:57:01.520
Andrew Rosener: Absolutely.&nbsp;
Absolutely. And that's just tip of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:01.520 --> 01:57:07.624
the iceberg. That's like the soft stuff. Yes.
Tim Ferriss: That's the Nerf baseball bat.&nbsp;

01:57:07.624 --> 01:57:11.120
Andrew Rosener: Exactly, exactly. Once you&nbsp;
get through the Nerf bat, there's another guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:11.120 --> 01:57:15.560
standing there with a real Louisville.
Tim Ferriss: So for yourself then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:15.560 --> 01:57:22.664
because you and I think are cut from similar&nbsp;
cloths in a number of respects, one of which —&nbsp;

