01:58:36.080 --> 01:58:41.440
problems for entrepreneurial success.
So the experience that I've had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:41.440 --> 01:58:47.800
and I think I've contended with to a large extent,&nbsp;
but because I have a high pain tolerance and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:47.800 --> 01:58:53.880
has been a competitive advantage, I've sometimes&nbsp;
ended up running towards painful things that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:53.880 --> 01:59:03.680
not worth doing. Which it seems like it can be&nbsp;
a necessary cost if you are pursuing worthwhile&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:03.680 --> 01:59:09.836
novelty, but just the fact that something is&nbsp;
painful does not justify its pursuit, right?&nbsp;

01:59:09.836 --> 01:59:10.560
Andrew Rosener: Absolutely not.
Tim Ferriss: So I'm wondering for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:10.560 --> 01:59:17.560
yourself how you navigate that, because even&nbsp;
now, I sometimes hear the siren song. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:17.560 --> 01:59:23.680
tempting because I've been rewarded for that in&nbsp;
the past. But as I get older, I'm like, all right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:23.680 --> 01:59:30.680
time is fleeting. I need to be more surgical about&nbsp;
how I approach these things, especially if I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:30.680 --> 01:59:37.464
trying to moderate my tendency towards anger,&nbsp;
which I think those two often go hand in hand.&nbsp;

01:59:37.464 --> 01:59:39.120
Andrew Rosener: Absolutely.
Tim Ferriss: Right? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:39.120 --> 01:59:42.200
how do you think about that?
Andrew Rosener: Simple. Very important&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:42.200 --> 01:59:47.880
to have a North Star, right? So what are you&nbsp;
optimizing for? What do you want? Right? For me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:47.880 --> 01:59:54.160
thanks to Charlie Tuna, I was lucky that I&nbsp;
learned very early on in my career, I had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:54.160 --> 01:59:59.400
let's say, a role model, not for everything,&nbsp;
but for certain things about how I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:59.400 --> 02:00:04.880
live my life. I thought, wow, if you were to&nbsp;
ask me when I was a junior in college, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:04.880 --> 02:00:07.400
"Oh, what do you want to be when you grow up?"&nbsp;
"I want to be like a, I don't know, billionaire&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:07.400 --> 02:00:11.360
tech entrepreneur with thousands of employees and&nbsp;
this huge company that everybody knows." And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:11.360 --> 02:00:15.560
it was like Charlie Tuna taught me like, whoa,&nbsp;
that's the opposite of what you want. No, no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:15.560 --> 02:00:21.920
no, no. Definitely, you want achieve financial&nbsp;
freedom, but that's just like step one. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:21.920 --> 02:00:29.160
like you want real freedom, you want free will.
And so I optimize for that. And so I decide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:29.160 --> 02:00:35.440
is this worth pursuing? Is this going to help me&nbsp;
achieve more or less freedom in my life? Right? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:35.440 --> 02:00:40.680
was about to build a business. We were going&nbsp;
to start a new company called Pegasus about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:40.680 --> 02:00:44.960
I don't know, a year ago now, six months,&nbsp;
a year ago, I don't know. And we spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:44.960 --> 02:00:52.040
six months planning this thing out,&nbsp;
got a CTO — we were ready to rock.&nbsp;

02:00:52.040 --> 02:00:56.960
And it was in the domain business. It wasn't&nbsp;
like I was venturing into something totally new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:56.960 --> 02:01:01.960
It's a business that I've never been so sure&nbsp;
I would've been successful in this business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:01.960 --> 02:01:05.800
I know it would've been successful. But we got&nbsp;
to the one-yard line, we were about to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:05.800 --> 02:01:11.480
and I asked myself, I was laying in bed and I'm&nbsp;
thinking to myself, so, literally tomorrow I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:11.480 --> 02:01:15.400
going to have this team meeting and we're going&nbsp;
to basically greenlight this and we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:15.400 --> 02:01:22.880
start putting this in action. And that means I'm&nbsp;
going to hire a bunch of people and that means I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:22.880 --> 02:01:30.280
going to be flying all over to have meetings.&nbsp;
And I was like, "I don't want any of that."&nbsp;

02:01:35.000 --> 02:01:40.680
There was a deep part of me that, I don't know&nbsp;
if imposter syndrome is the right word. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:40.680 --> 02:01:47.520
achieved a pretty high degree of, let's&nbsp;
say, financial freedom. But my business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:47.520 --> 02:01:53.160
most of your audience would probably say my&nbsp;
business is garbage. Like, ah. And the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:53.160 --> 02:01:59.480
being is I can't scale it. I can't sell it.
Tim Ferriss: You might be surprised, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:00.080 --> 02:02:01.622
understand what you're saying.
Andrew Rosener: Maybe.&nbsp;

02:02:01.622 --> 02:02:04.240
Tim Ferriss: But, there will be a subset.
Andrew Rosener: Totally. Traditional metrics&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:04.240 --> 02:02:09.840
of let's say, what makes a good business.&nbsp;
My business doesn't meet that description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:09.840 --> 02:02:15.280
but my business is amazing for me, and I&nbsp;
don't want it to be for somebody else. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:15.280 --> 02:02:21.160
want it to be for me. And so I run it the&nbsp;
way I want to run it. And it brings me back&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:21.160 --> 02:02:26.040
to a tenet of Charlie Tuna. If you pay for the&nbsp;
microphone, you get to say what you want to say.&nbsp;

02:02:26.040 --> 02:02:30.240
Tim Ferriss: That resonates for me.
Andrew Rosener: And that comes from, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:30.240 --> 02:02:35.880
it was Ronald Reagan who said it in a presidential&nbsp;
debate. He said, or no, I think it was George&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:35.880 --> 02:02:44.800
Bush, Sr. And he said, "Excuse me, but I paid for&nbsp;
this microphone." Somebody tried to cut him off.&nbsp;

02:02:44.800 --> 02:02:50.360
Anyways, I optimize for freedom. I optimize&nbsp;
for freedom of deciding what to do with my&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:50.360 --> 02:02:56.400
time outside of email. I optimize for being&nbsp;
able to live where I want to live. I optimize&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:56.400 --> 02:03:01.760
for being able to move if I want to move. I&nbsp;
