Now, for simplicity's sake, I'm focusing on Google&nbsp;
here because it's the one that people are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:10.720 --> 02:18:15.520
resonate with the most, but we extrapolated —
Not immediately, but over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:15.520 --> 02:18:23.081
we've extrapolated this out to basically&nbsp;
measuring use in culture and use in commerce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:23.080 --> 02:18:27.119
which can be measured on social media, which can&nbsp;
be measured on YouTube, which can be measured&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:27.119 --> 02:18:35.080
across a whole frame of different metrics. But for&nbsp;
the purpose of simplicity, we were measuring how&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:35.080 --> 02:18:40.800
many people are searching Google for this keyword&nbsp;
or acronym? What are advertisers willing to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:40.800 --> 02:18:45.720
for that keyword or acronym? And that's going to&nbsp;
give you, just those two numbers alone is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:45.720 --> 02:18:50.920
to tell you what's the total addressable market&nbsp;
on a monthly basis for that keyword or acronym&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:50.920 --> 02:18:56.159
objectively. And then you can extrapolate that&nbsp;
out by a reasonable business multiple and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:56.159 --> 02:19:03.519
"Okay, well this total market is worth, I don't&nbsp;
know, conservatively, let's say 36 months." In&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:03.520 --> 02:19:09.667
some other industries, maybe it's 60 months. In&nbsp;
some other industries, it might be 120 months.&nbsp;

02:19:09.667 --> 02:19:14.640
Tim Ferriss: Can you explain that for a second?&nbsp;
I'm from Long Island. A little slow on the uptake&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:14.640 --> 02:19:19.000
sometimes. So explain how the time varies.
Andrew Rosener:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:19.000 --> 02:19:19.664
Okay, so each business or industry —
Tim Ferriss: Or duration, so to speak.&nbsp;

02:19:19.664 --> 02:19:26.720
Andrew Rosener: Each business or industry, if&nbsp;
you're looking at M&amp;A, one business is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:26.720 --> 02:19:30.920
buy another business, there's generally a multiple&nbsp;
that these people will pay. In each industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:30.920 --> 02:19:33.680
it varies. And the type of business varies.
Tim Ferriss: I see what you're saying. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:33.680 --> 02:19:39.080
multiples is dependent on which industry&nbsp;
the search term is associated with.&nbsp;

02:19:39.080 --> 02:19:40.840
Andrew Rosener: Right. How&nbsp;
durable is this business?&nbsp;

02:19:40.840 --> 02:19:48.464
Tim Ferriss: I got it, I got it. So if&nbsp;
it's retail versus SaaS versus whatever.&nbsp;

02:19:48.464 --> 02:19:54.200
Andrew Rosener: Yeah. If it's some sort of the&nbsp;
hot, new shiny object trend, you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:54.199 --> 02:19:58.880
put a pretty low multiple on it. It might be&nbsp;
two years, three years. If it's, I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:58.880 --> 02:20:05.760
cabinets.com, okay. It might be a very long&nbsp;
multiple because it's a very durable industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:05.760 --> 02:20:11.560
It's not going to be a lot of disruption, and the&nbsp;
multiple's going to generally be a bit higher.&nbsp;

02:20:11.560 --> 02:20:15.600
Tim Ferriss: I see. I wasn't thinking about&nbsp;
this correctly. So the durability is a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:15.600 --> 02:20:21.800
factor in this particular calculation. It's&nbsp;
not just looking at the industry and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:21.800 --> 02:20:26.080
type of acquisition prices —
Andrew Rosener: It's both.&nbsp;

02:20:26.080 --> 02:20:30.503
Tim Ferriss: Right. It's a&nbsp;
combination of those, totally.&nbsp;

02:20:30.504 --> 02:20:34.480
Andrew Rosener: And again, this comes back to&nbsp;
meeting people where they are. So I generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:34.480 --> 02:20:40.001
will not presume, and I'll ask them, "If you were&nbsp;
buying a business in your industry, what's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:40.000 --> 02:20:47.520
multiple you'd be looking to pay?" And that's the&nbsp;
number that I'll use. And most people are — they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:47.520 --> 02:20:54.520
know that off the top of their head, like,&nbsp;
"Generally, we pay 3X, 5X." So when I'm doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:54.520 --> 02:21:00.400
back of the napkin, I generally use three because&nbsp;
I like to just be conservative, and I find that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:00.400 --> 02:21:05.480
be the best approach with domain names. Because&nbsp;
as you said, most people don't understand how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:05.480 --> 02:21:09.120
value these things. So the more conservative&nbsp;
you are, the more you're going to resonate.&nbsp;

02:21:09.119 --> 02:21:14.800
So you multiply that out, you got how many people&nbsp;
search per month. Okay, let's just call it use in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:14.800 --> 02:21:20.480
commerce and culture. We can measure that. What&nbsp;
are people willing to pay for that traffic? We&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:20.480 --> 02:21:25.600
can measure that, multiply those two, and then&nbsp;
extrapolate out by the number of months that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:25.600 --> 02:21:30.680
appropriate for that industry or business. That's&nbsp;
going to tell you basically, roughly speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:30.680 --> 02:21:33.680
there's other variables, but that's going&nbsp;
to tell you your total addressable market.&nbsp;

02:21:35.360 --> 02:21:41.480
We're going to look at click-through rate and&nbsp;
conversion rate. And so that's the next part&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:41.480 --> 02:21:46.960
of the formula is here's the total addressable&nbsp;
market, and here's the amount of that market I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:46.960 --> 02:21:52.880
feel like I can realistically capture. And this&nbsp;
is where sort of the SEO comes in. So if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:52.880 --> 02:21:57.920
ranking number one in Google for a particular&nbsp;
keyword, and it varies based on keyword,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:57.920 --> 02:22:03.359
and you're the number one organic listing,&nbsp;
meaning you're not paying Google to be number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:03.360 --> 02:22:11.400
you're organically ranking, you can reasonably&nbsp;
expect somewhere in the 25 percent call. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:11.400 --> 02:22:18.400
used to be 28, maybe it's 23, maybe it's 18,&nbsp;
but it's 20, 28 percent click-through rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:18.400 --> 02:22:22.280
just for being the organic first listing of that.
Okay, so let's just say roughly speaking, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:22.279 --> 02:22:26.920
a hundred thousand people a month searching for&nbsp;
this keyword. 28 percent of those paid, let's say&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:26.920 --> 02:22:31.440
25 free easy numbers because I'm doing public&nbsp;
math, which is generally a rule I have against&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:31.440 --> 02:22:36.840
doing, but 25 percent of those people are going&nbsp;
to click. So now you've got 25 thousand people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:36.840 --> 02:22:42.000
that are going to come to your website, and how&nbsp;
many of those people are you going to reasonably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:42.000 --> 02:22:48.600
convert? And I like to use two percent. I think&nbsp;
it's generally a conservative e-commerce metric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:48.600 --> 02:22:58.560
two to four percent, but let's say two percent.&nbsp;
So you're going to have about 500 people per month&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:58.560 --> 02:23:06.160
that are converting. And then you multiply that&nbsp;
by whatever you would have been. It's basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:06.159 --> 02:23:10.960
the opportunity cost. So what would you have been&nbsp;
paying Google to get that? Okay. And that's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:10.960 --> 02:23:16.800
to tell you the cost of customer acquisition&nbsp;
to be that number one position in Google.&nbsp;

02:23:16.800 --> 02:23:22.920
And then based on a bunch of studies from&nbsp;
Microsoft, and we can reference those studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:22.920 --> 02:23:28.440
in your show notes if you want, but you can&nbsp;
demonstrate what the delta is between having&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:28.440 --> 02:23:33.240
a domain name that clearly — it needs to be&nbsp;
one of two things. It needs to be a brand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:33.239 --> 02:23:43.279
people trust and/or recognize. Or it needs to&nbsp;
have clear intent baked in. So the domain name&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:43.279 --> 02:23:49.279
needs to match the intent of the user. And if you&nbsp;
have those two things, you're going to materially&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:49.279 --> 02:23:54.239
increase — I don't remember the exact number&nbsp;
offhand, but it's a very material increase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:54.239 --> 02:24:00.479
the conversion rate and the click-through rate,&nbsp;
both. So using that formula, we can demonstrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:00.480 --> 02:24:08.720
very clearly the delta between your cost of&nbsp;
customer acquisition on joeysbagofdonuts.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:08.720 --> 02:24:16.320
versus donuts.com. You're going to have the&nbsp;
higher click-through rate, which is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:16.320 --> 02:24:20.360
to give you the bigger opportunity, and then&nbsp;
you're going to have a higher conversion rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:20.360 --> 02:24:28.480
which is going to obviously grow your business.&nbsp;
And so that delta extrapolated out by whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:28.480 --> 02:24:35.147
that reasonable business multiple is the objective&nbsp;
value of the domain name. Does that make sense?&nbsp;

02:24:35.146 --> 02:24:38.263
Tim Ferriss: It does make sense. I think&nbsp;
I'd probably need to see it on paper.&nbsp;

02:24:38.263 --> 02:24:42.199
Andrew Rosener: So ultimately, we're&nbsp;
taking it back to that first principle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:42.199 --> 02:24:53.199
which is a domain name’ss value is its ability to&nbsp;
arbitrage your cost of customer acquisition. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:53.199 --> 02:25:01.399
can reduce your cost of customer acquisition with&nbsp;
this domain name, there's a value. And then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:01.400 --> 02:25:06.961
just have to multiply that value out by however&nbsp;
long you feel is appropriate for your business&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:06.960 --> 02:25:13.359
to tell you what is this domain name worth&nbsp;
to your business. And that is a non-trivial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:13.360 --> 02:25:19.600
non-objectionable business case that ultimately&nbsp;
is what led to my success in this business was I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:19.600 --> 02:25:27.880
was able to make a business case and convert&nbsp;
people where others failed. Because it was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:27.880 --> 02:25:34.400
mathematical valuation methodology that nobody&nbsp;
had ever seen before. And you could say, "Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:35.480 --> 02:25:40.720
how can you say no? I'm showing you how I'm going&nbsp;
to reduce your cost of customer acquisition, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:40.720 --> 02:25:47.720
is one of the most important metrics any business&nbsp;
is going to measure. And so if I can do that, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:47.720 --> 02:25:51.800
many months multiple are you willing to pay me?"
Tim Ferriss: I guess, the months multiple is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:51.800 --> 02:25:54.960
where it seems like you could have a disagreement.
Andrew Rosener: Sure, absolutely. And that's where&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:54.960 --> 02:26:03.840
the disagreement lies. But it certainly doesn't&nbsp;
have a hundred percent hit rate. Most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:03.840 --> 02:26:08.720
still have an emotional block to, "I'm not going&nbsp;
to pay some domain squatters X amount of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:08.720 --> 02:26:13.840
for this domain name." It's just an emotional&nbsp;
wall. And sometimes I can break through that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:13.840 --> 02:26:20.360
sometimes I can't. Over long periods of time, I'm&nbsp;
generally pretty good at converting people. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:21.760 --> 02:26:28.239
once it clicks for people that there is a material&nbsp;
reduction in the cost of a customer acquisition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:28.239 --> 02:26:34.239
I don't need closed deal on that day. It's like&nbsp;
the movie Inception, the Leonardo DiCaprio movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:35.079 --> 02:26:39.920
Once I've planted the seed in your mind, you're&nbsp;
going to go home and you're going to go to bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:39.920 --> 02:26:42.319
you're going to have board meetings,&nbsp;
you're going to meet with your founders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:42.320 --> 02:26:48.800
you're going to meet with teams in your company,&nbsp;
and at some point it's going to come up and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:48.800 --> 02:26:54.760
going to wake up one morning with this moment&nbsp;
of inception where you think it's your idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:56.520 --> 02:27:00.600
you're going to be able to scale your business&nbsp;
in a way that you otherwise wouldn't be able to.&nbsp;

02:27:00.600 --> 02:27:07.240
You're going to launch a new division, you're&nbsp;
going to start buying paid advertising. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:07.239 --> 02:27:12.719
initiative is going to benefit tremendously by&nbsp;
owning this domain name. Maybe a new competitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:12.720 --> 02:27:16.480
has launched. Maybe there's somebody with&nbsp;
your brand name in a different industry that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:16.480 --> 02:27:20.840
you don't want them to have the domain,&nbsp;
but something is going to pop. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:20.840 --> 02:27:25.880
going to come a moment — which is why domain&nbsp;
names are generally a very long sales cycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:25.880 --> 02:27:29.960
but there's going to be an inception point&nbsp;
where you wake up one morning and you think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:29.960 --> 02:27:34.680
"Okay, now I need this domain name." And then&nbsp;
you're going to call me back, or you're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:34.680 --> 02:27:40.240
send me an email and you're going to say, "Hey, is&nbsp;
that domain still available?" And sometimes it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:40.239 --> 02:27:45.519
sometimes it's not. Another sort of&nbsp;
powerful phrase that we've coined is off&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:45.520 --> 02:27:50.480
the market forever. Once these things sell,&nbsp;
they're generally off the market forever.&nbsp;

02:27:50.480 --> 02:27:54.720
The moment a company makes a decision to acquire&nbsp;
this domain name that they were fighting tooth&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:54.720 --> 02:28:03.720
and nail not to buy previously, it's basically&nbsp;
priceless to them. Because what they then say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:03.720 --> 02:28:10.079
which is so interesting to me because they don't&nbsp;
realize it before buying it, is that they then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:10.079 --> 02:28:17.960
say, "But this is my brand." And you go, well, "I&nbsp;
couldn't agree more, but then why didn't you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:17.960 --> 02:28:23.520
to buy it in the first place?" But the moment&nbsp;
a company buys it, the first thing they'll tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:23.520 --> 02:28:27.680
you is, "This is my brand. I can't sell it."
I won't use this specific example, but we sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:27.680 --> 02:28:32.960
the domain. It was quite a good price, but I was&nbsp;
fighting tooth and nail. Nobody wanted to buy this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:32.960 --> 02:28:37.399
domain. It was a very good domain. The price was&nbsp;
very good. It was $250,000 for a domain that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:37.400 --> 02:28:43.480
would tell you was worth at least double, triple&nbsp;
that. And we finally sold it. And immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:43.480 --> 02:28:49.520
literally 24 hours later, I would say the David&nbsp;
versus the Goliath, the David shows up and says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:49.520 --> 02:28:54.320
"You know what? I made a mistake. I want to buy&nbsp;
it." And said, "I just sold it." And so this guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:54.320 --> 02:28:58.640
was really, really successful, but he was&nbsp;
like a ninja versus a big Goliath. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:58.640 --> 02:29:04.040
like the Charlie Tuna versus the big factory.
But he had a really successful business and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:04.040 --> 02:29:08.119
had some money. And he said, "Look, offer them..."&nbsp;
I think it was — like the example, I think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:08.119 --> 02:29:12.559
a hundred thousand dollars more. Owner said no.&nbsp;
And this guy had just bought it. He hadn't done&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:12.560 --> 02:29:17.080
a single thing with the domain name. It literally&nbsp;
hit his account 24 hours ago. He hasn't set up an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:17.079 --> 02:29:21.800
email on it. He hasn't even redirected the domain&nbsp;
name. It's got a parking page on it, nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:21.800 --> 02:29:28.000
zero. He just is now the owner. And we went from&nbsp;
— he bought it for 250. This guy offered 350. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:28.000 --> 02:29:34.840
said no. Went to 500. He said no. Went to 750.&nbsp;
He said no. Went to $1 million. He said no. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:34.840 --> 02:29:41.239
guy tapped out. And this guy was just like, "I&nbsp;
don't think there's a price. It would basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:41.239 --> 02:29:47.399
be he'd have to buy my whole business for me&nbsp;
to sell this domain because this is my brand."&nbsp;

02:29:47.400 --> 02:29:52.120
And it's a very strange phenomena where&nbsp;
people don't — they don't make that click&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:52.119 --> 02:29:58.159
before buying the domain. And the moment they&nbsp;
own it, they realize how powerful this is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:58.159 --> 02:30:02.840
their brand. It's the most important&nbsp;
asset in many cases, not in all cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:02.840 --> 02:30:08.480
but in many cases it's much more valuable and much&nbsp;
more powerful than their trademark itself. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:08.480 --> 02:30:14.920
he who has the dot com, there's only one. And so&nbsp;
Apple wouldn't be — if it was Applecomputer.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:14.920 --> 02:30:20.520
they couldn't run around calling themselves Apple&nbsp;
because somebody else would own Apple. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:20.520 --> 02:30:26.520
would be Apple because the dot com is ingrained&nbsp;
into people's brains with trillions and trillions&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:26.520 --> 02:30:31.200
and trillions of dollars in advertising since&nbsp;
1985 when the first domain name was registered.&nbsp;

02:30:31.199 --> 02:30:36.239
It is just an endless — every sports game&nbsp;
has domain names pasted around the arena.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:36.760 --> 02:30:42.400
Every news channel, you're getting inundated&nbsp;
with dot coms, every big brand that you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:42.400 --> 02:30:48.280
it is just ingrained. It's assumed. And&nbsp;
so if you are anything except your exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:48.279 --> 02:30:57.519
brand match dot com, you cannot or should&nbsp;
not refer to yourself as your, let's say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:57.520 --> 02:31:05.800
raw brand. If you're dot net or dot io or dot&nbsp;
AI even, I would argue, and you're not calling&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:05.800 --> 02:31:09.360
yourself — let's say you're, I'll use Dharmesh&nbsp;
because he's a good sport. Dharmesh from HubSpot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:09.360 --> 02:31:16.200
He has agent.ai. It's a new little venture that&nbsp;
he's launched. And you can't call yourself Agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:16.199 --> 02:31:21.423
unless you've got agent.com, because —
Tim Ferriss: Why not? Just to —&nbsp;

02:31:21.424 --> 02:31:25.400
Andrew Rosener: Because people are going to&nbsp;
assume you're dot com. If you're agent.ai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:25.400 --> 02:31:33.400
then your brand is agent.ai. And if you refer to&nbsp;
yourself as anything else, at a minimum, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:33.400 --> 02:31:37.961
going to lose 10 percent of your traffic. This&nbsp;
has been done. The best study is from Overstock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:37.960 --> 02:31:46.000
Overstock tried to do a rebrand to o.co. That's&nbsp;
a whole super phenomenal, interesting story that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:46.000 --> 02:31:53.239
comes back to the one letter dot coms and whatnot.&nbsp;
But anyways, they tried to do a rebrand to o.co,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:53.239 --> 02:31:59.559
and they were obsessed with this, and they'd&nbsp;
made an enormous campaign, and they did massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:59.560 --> 02:32:06.960
branding, massive advertising to promote this&nbsp;
rebrand. And they immediately — I don't remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:06.960 --> 02:32:13.600
I think it was like six months, maybe less, they&nbsp;
immediately pivoted back to overstock.com because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:13.600 --> 02:32:18.240
what they saw was that they were losing,&nbsp;
I think it was 40 percent, it's like 30 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:18.239 --> 02:32:24.199
40 percent of all their traffic was getting lost.
Tim Ferriss: And why didn't they just redirect&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:24.199 --> 02:32:31.439
Overstock.com to o.co? Is that a dumb question?
Andrew Rosener: Well, no, the point was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:31.440 --> 02:32:38.387
they're out there advertising themselves as&nbsp;
Overstock is now O, but then people type in o.com.&nbsp;

02:32:38.387 --> 02:32:40.544
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I see what you're&nbsp;
saying. I see what you mean.&nbsp;

02:32:40.544 --> 02:32:46.680
Andrew Rosener: And so then it's a dead website.&nbsp;
So what does that say about your brand. Like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:46.680 --> 02:32:53.011
I am on a 404 page, or I'm on a dead&nbsp;
— this domain doesn't resolve page."&nbsp;

02:32:53.011 --> 02:32:56.960
This domain doesn't resolve, so it's&nbsp;
very detrimental to your brand. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:56.960 --> 02:33:02.199
Amazon highlights this best. I don't know&nbsp;
what the increase in conversion rate was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:02.199 --> 02:33:07.679
but like just by taking away one step&nbsp;
and making the one click purchase had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:07.680 --> 02:33:13.664
a profound impact on conversion for Amazon.
Tim Ferriss: Say that one more time please.&nbsp;

02:33:13.664 --> 02:33:16.146
Andrew Rosener: When Amazon&nbsp;
introduced the one click purchasing —&nbsp;

02:33:16.146 --> 02:33:18.519
Tim Ferriss: Oh, sure.
Andrew Rosener: — it had a profound impact.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:19.840 --> 02:33:26.319
I remember at the time they were talking about&nbsp;
it like these very small — if you can remove just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:26.320 --> 02:33:34.360
even very small incremental elements of friction&nbsp;
between your customer in their journey to close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:35.159 --> 02:33:40.239
every small bit of friction that you can remove&nbsp;
you're materially increasing your conversion rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:40.239 --> 02:33:44.719
and obviously top line and bottom line.
Tim Ferriss: As demonstrated by the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:44.720 --> 02:33:51.159
of Amazon boxes that arrive based on my 3:00 AM&nbsp;
orders that I think are 100 percent important and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:51.159 --> 02:33:58.079
critical to my lifestyle. So ultimately that's&nbsp;
